/* mcq-why.js — "Why not the others?" per-distractor rationale for theory MCQs.
   Keyed by exact question text -> { exact wrong-option text: rationale }. Consumed by
   whyNotHtml() in the app (window.MCQWHY). Generated/merged from authored chunks; every
   key is validated against the live MCQ bank at merge time. Generator-backed (math)
   questions are intentionally excluded (their option text changes each serve). */
window.MCQWHY={"Which of the following is NOT considered &ldquo;Financial Advice&rdquo; under the Standards?":{"Recommending the client buy a specific mutual fund":"A specific product recommendation IS Financial Advice — the very definition. The question asks what is NOT advice.","Exercising discretionary authority over the account":"Exercising discretion is explicitly Financial Advice under the Standards, not an exclusion.","Recommending the client refrain from selling a stock":"A recommendation to hold/not sell is still a specific recommendation, so it IS Financial Advice."},"A CFP® professional lacks competence in a needed area. Which is NOT an acceptable response under Standard A.3?":{"Gain the necessary competence":"Gaining competence is an explicitly permitted response under A.3, so it is acceptable.","Limit the scope of the engagement":"Limiting scope to areas of competence is one of the acceptable A.3 responses.","Refer the client to another professional":"Referring to a competent professional is a permitted A.3 response, not a violation."},"A planner charges planning fees AND earns commissions on insurance products sold. This compensation is:":{"Fee-only":"Fee-only means planning fees ONLY — no commissions. Earning insurance commissions disqualifies fee-only.","Commission-only prohibited":"Commission-only is not prohibited, and this planner also charges fees, so it isn't commission-only.","Non-compensable":"Not a real compensation category; the planner is clearly being compensated by both fees and commissions."},"Under Standard A.15, a CFP® professional may lend to or borrow from a client only when the client is:":{"A long-time friend":"Friendship is not an exception; A.15's only carve-outs are a family member or a lending-business entity.","Any client who signs a waiver":"A client waiver does not create an exception — the no-lending rule cannot be waived that way.","A fellow CFP® professional":"Being a fellow CFP® professional is not one of the two A.15 exceptions."},"How many days does a CFP® professional have to report adverse conduct to the CFP Board?":{"14 days":"14 days is the deadline to respond to document/information requests, not to self-report adverse conduct.","45 days":"45 days applies to interim-suspension compliance, not adverse-conduct reporting.","90 days":"Not a CFP Board reporting deadline; adverse conduct must be reported within 30 days."},"The three core components of a CFP® professional&rsquo;s fiduciary duty are loyalty, care, and:":{"Disclosure":"Disclosure is part of managing conflicts (A.5), not the third fiduciary duty. The third duty is to Follow Client Instructions.","Confidentiality":"Confidentiality is its own standard (A.9), not a component of the A.1 fiduciary duty.","Diligence":"Diligence is a separate standard (A.2), not part of the three-part fiduciary duty."},"Which is a hallmark of the SUITABILITY standard (vs. the fiduciary standard)?":{"Written disclosure and best-interest obligation":"Written disclosure and best-interest are hallmarks of the FIDUCIARY standard, not suitability.","Applies to RIAs and ERISA fiduciaries":"RIAs and ERISA fiduciaries are held to the fiduciary standard, not the suitability standard.","Solution-driven and principle-based":"Principle/solution-driven describes the fiduciary approach; suitability is rules-based and product-driven."},"A CFP® Board Hearing Panel must contain at least:":{"5 people, all CFP® professionals":"Wrong count and composition — minimum is 3, and it must include a DEC member, not be all CFP® pros.","2 people, both DEC members":"Too few and wrong makeup — minimum 3 with at least 2 CFP® professionals and 1 DEC member.","3 people, all attorneys":"Correct count but wrong composition — the panel needs CFP® professionals and a DEC member, not all attorneys."},"Which sanction is the only one that is NOT publicly disseminated?":{"Public Censure":"By definition a Public Censure is published; it is publicly disseminated.","Suspension":"Suspensions are publicly disseminated sanctions.","Revocation":"Revocation is publicly disseminated — the most severe public sanction."},"A felony conviction for embezzlement results in which CFP Board outcome?":{"Must file a petition for fitness":"A petition applies to presumptive-bar conduct; embezzlement is an ABSOLUTE bar, so no petition path exists.","Currently ineligible, then proceed":"That's the presumptive/temporary category; embezzlement is a permanent absolute bar.","Private censure":"Far too lenient — felony embezzlement is an absolute bar from certification, not a censure."},"A planner must respond to CFP Board document/information requests within how many days?":{"30 days":"30 days is to acknowledge an investigation or answer a complaint, not to respond to document requests (14 days).","45 days":"45 days is the interim-suspension compliance window, not the document-request deadline.","60 days":"Not a CFP Board deadline; document/information requests are due in 14 calendar days."},"More integration factors being present in a situation makes it:":{"Less likely that Financial Planning is required":"Backwards — more integration factors signal greater complexity, making planning MORE likely required.","Irrelevant to the planning requirement":"Integration factors are precisely how the requirement is gauged; they are not irrelevant.","A violation of the Code of Ethics":"Complexity factors don't create a violation — they only indicate whether Financial Planning applies."},"Choose the goal-related action(s) that occur in Step 2 of the Practice Standards. I. Identifying Potential Goals II. Selecting and Prioritizing Goals III. Collaboration to establish personal and economic assumptions IV. Discussion of unrealistic goals":{"I only":"Too narrow — Step 2 includes selecting/prioritizing goals, establishing assumptions, and addressing unrealistic goals too.","I and II":"Omits establishing assumptions (III) and discussing unrealistic goals (IV), which also occur in Step 2.","II, III, and IV":"Wrongly drops I (identifying potential goals), which is part of Step 2 — all four apply."},"Mateo, a CFP® professional, created a financial plan for his client, Emma, a single mother, who asked him to provide recommendations regarding her retirement. During that meeting, Mateo asked Emma if she would like a life insurance analysis. Emma declined, saying she was currently only interested in investing for her retirement. In the letter of engagement, signed by both parties, Mateo noted this. After discussing Emma’s retirement goals, needs, and priorities, and gathering her retirement information, Mateo set another appointment with Emma in which he would present a financial plan for her. Several weeks later, Mateo met with Emma to discuss her financial plan. In it, he provided two recommendations: (1) that Emma purchase an annuity to save for her retirement, and (2) that she purchase a life insurance policy so her young children would be financially secure in the event of her death. Neither recommendation was discussed in the plan. Emma advised Mateo that she does not like annuities as an investment and restated that she’s not interested in life insurance. How were Mateo’s recommendations in the plan inconsistent with the Practice Standards, Step 4: Developing the Financial Planning Recommendations? I. He failed to adequately define the scope of his engagement with Emma. II. He did not offer alternatives to the annuity for Emma’s retirement investment. III. He did not stay within the scope of the engagement by recommending life insurance for Emma. IV. In the plan, he failed to explain how the recommendation is designed to maximize the potential of Emma meeting her retirement goals.":{"I and III":"Statement I is false — the signed letter of engagement DID define scope; the failures are II, III, and IV.","I, II, and III":"Includes the false Statement I and omits IV (failing to explain how the recommendation maximizes goal attainment).","I, II, III, and IV 20":"Statement I is incorrect — scope WAS adequately defined by the engagement letter, so the answer is II, III, and IV only."},"Five years ago, Stuart invested $125,000 in Collegiate Construction":{"Endowment":"Endowment bias is overvaluing an asset merely because you already own it; Stuart's bias stems from emotional identification, not ownership.","Self-control":"Self-control bias is favoring immediate gratification over long-term goals — unrelated to Stuart's loyalty to the stock.","Overconfidence":"Overconfidence is overrating one's knowledge/control; Stuart's pull is emotional affinity with his alma mater, not overrated ability."},"Which approach to implementing the fiduciary standard has been adopted by the CFP Board and RIAs?":{"Process-based approach":"Not one of the two recognized approaches — the choice is rules-based vs. principle-based; RIAs/CFP Board use principle-based.","Rules-based approach":"Rules-based is the OTHER approach (used for suitability); the CFP Board and RIAs adopted the principle-based approach.","Standardized approach":"Not a recognized fiduciary-implementation approach; the correct term is principle-based."},"Which of the following is NOT a component of the Fiduciary Duty (Standard A.1) in the Duties Owed to Clients section of the Standards of Conduct?":{"Duty of Loyalty":"Duty of Loyalty IS one of the three A.1 fiduciary duties, so it is not the exception.","Duty to Follow Client Instructions":"This is a genuine A.1 fiduciary duty, so it belongs — the odd one out is Duty to Disclose.","Duty of Care 221":"Duty of Care is one of the three A.1 fiduciary duties, so it is not the exception."},"Identify the transgressions that are presumed to be unacceptable according to the CFP Board’s Fitness Standards. I. Felony conviction for tax fraud or other tax-related crimes II. Felony conviction for nonviolent crimes (including perjury) within the last five years III. Revocation of a financial professional license that is administrative in nature IV. Two or more personal or business bankruptcies":{"I and II":"Statement I (felony tax fraud) is an ABSOLUTE bar, not merely presumed unacceptable; the presumptive items are II and IV.","I and III":"Both wrong — I is an absolute bar and III is only administrative (not presumptive); the answer is II and IV.","I, II, and IV":"Includes I, which is an absolute bar rather than a presumptive one; correct presumptive set is II and IV."},"Identify the parties involved in the disciplinary and appeals process that have representation of at least one CFP® professional. I. Enforcement Counsel II. Hearing Panel III. Disciplinary and Ethics Committee IV. Appeals Committee":{"I only":"Enforcement Counsel isn't the CFP®-represented body; the Hearing Panel, DEC, and Appeals Committee are.","III only":"Too narrow — the Hearing Panel and Appeals Committee also include CFP® professionals, not just the DEC.","I and III":"Enforcement Counsel (I) prosecutes and isn't the CFP®-membership body; the answer set is II, III, and IV."},"All of the following forms of sanctions can be issued to current CFP® professionals except":{"revocation.":"Revocation applies to CURRENT CFP® professionals, so it is not the exception.","suspension.":"Suspension is a sanction for current CFP® professionals, so it isn't the exception.","private censure.":"Private Censure applies to current CFP® professionals; the permanent bar is what applies to non-certificants."},"Terrance, a CFP® professional, had one of his clients recently file a complaint alleging that he transferred money into unsuitable investments. During the course of the investigation, Terrance was not forthcoming and was uncooperative. All of the following could be grounds for discipline except":{"Terrance failing to respond to a request by the Disciplinary and Ethics Commission without good cause.":"Failing to respond without good cause IS grounds for discipline, so it is not the exception.","Terrance obstructing the Disciplinary and Ethics Commission’s performance of its duties.":"Obstructing the DEC is clearly disciplinable, so it isn't the exception.","Terrance providing false or misleading statements to the CFP Board.":"Providing false/misleading statements is grounds for discipline; merely disagreeing with findings is not."},"Under Standard A.1, the Fiduciary Duty owed when providing Financial Advice consists of which three duties?":{"Integrity, Competence, and Diligence":"These are separate standalone standards (A.2–A.4 area), not the three A.1 fiduciary duties.","Disclosure, Consent, and Management":"That's the conflict-of-interest process (A.5), not the fiduciary duty's three components.","Loyalty, Confidentiality, and Professionalism":"Only Loyalty belongs; Confidentiality (A.9) and Professionalism are separate standards, not fiduciary duties."},"A CFP&reg; professional sincerely believes a recommendation is in the client's best interest despite a related compensation conflict. Under Standard A.5, the conflict must be:":{"Disclosed only if the client asks":"Disclosure is mandatory and proactive; it is not contingent on the client asking.","Ignored because the planner is acting in the client's best interest":"A sincere best-interest belief does NOT excuse disclosure — material conflicts must still be disclosed and managed.","Disclosed only after implementation":"Disclosure and informed consent must come before/at recommendation, not after implementation."},"Which disclosure may be delivered either verbally OR in writing in both Financial Advice and Financial Planning engagements?":{"Privacy policy":"The privacy policy must be delivered in writing, not verbally.","Public discipline and bankruptcy":"Public discipline and bankruptcy disclosures must be in writing.","How the firm and related parties are compensated":"Compensation disclosure must be in writing, not merely verbal."},"According to the Practice Standards, all of the following are approved documentation methods EXCEPT:":{"CRM software":"CRM software is an approved documentation method, so it is not the exception.","Handwritten notes":"Handwritten notes are an approved documentation method.","Emails":"Emails are an approved documentation method; voice memos are the one not listed."},"In data gathering, which of the following is QUALITATIVE (subjective) rather than quantitative data?":{"Section 401(k) account statement":"An account statement is hard, objective (quantitative) data, not subjective.","Income tax return":"A tax return is objective quantitative data — numbers and facts, not subjective.","Liability/debt statement":"A debt statement is quantitative (objective) data, not qualitative."},"If a planner cannot obtain sufficient and relevant quantitative information to form a basis for recommendations, the planner must:":{"Proceed using reasonable assumptions for the missing data":"You can't fabricate a basis with assumptions for missing core data; you must restrict scope or terminate.","Report the client to CFP Board":"A client withholding information is not a reportable violation; the planner restricts scope or terminates.","Obtain the information from a third party without client consent":"Gathering data without client consent breaches confidentiality; the fix is to limit scope or terminate."},"Under Code &amp; Standards A.10.b.ii, who is responsible for implementing Financial Planning recommendations?":{"The client, always":"Not always the client — the planner is responsible unless specifically excluded from the Scope of Engagement.","A third-party custodian":"A custodian merely holds assets; implementation responsibility rests with the planner unless scoped out.","CFP Board":"The CFP Board sets standards; it never implements client recommendations."},"A CFP&reg; professional is asked to provide a service for which she lacks sufficient competence. Standard A.3 permits all of the following responses EXCEPT:":{"Gain the necessary competence":"Gaining competence is an explicitly permitted A.3 response, so it isn't the exception.","Limit the scope of the engagement":"Limiting scope to competent areas is a permitted A.3 response.","Refer the client to a competent professional":"Referring to a competent professional is permitted under A.3; proceeding with a disclaimer is not."},"A firm receives only financial planning fees and no commissions or sales-related compensation. Under Standard A.12, this firm is:":{"Fee-based":"Fee-based requires commissions/sales compensation ADDED to fees; this firm takes fees only, so it's fee-only.","Commission-based":"The firm earns no commissions at all, so it cannot be commission-based.","Fee-offset":"Fee-offset reduces fees by commissions earned — but this firm earns no commissions, so the term doesn't apply."},"Under Standard A.14, when relying on technology to provide services, a CFP&reg; professional must:":{"Be a technology expert":"A.14 requires only reasonable understanding, not expert-level technical mastery.","Avoid all third-party software":"A.14 permits reliance on technology; it doesn't ban third-party software.","Personally audit the source code":"Reasonable diligence is required, not a personal source-code audit."},"When recommending another professional to a client, Standard A.13 requires the CFP&reg; professional to do all of the following EXCEPT:":{"Have a reasonable basis for the referral":"A reasonable basis IS required by A.13, so it is not the exception.","Disclose compensation paid by anyone other than the client":"Disclosing third-party referral compensation is required by A.13.","Communicate the respective roles and inform the client of non-performance":"Communicating roles and reporting non-performance are A.13 requirements; guaranteeing performance is not."},"Which is NOT one of the three stated purposes of the Code of Ethics and Standards of Conduct?":{"Benefit and protect the public":"This IS one of the three stated purposes, so it isn't the exception.","Provide standards for delivering financial planning":"Providing delivery standards is a stated purpose of the Code.","Advance financial planning as a distinct and valuable profession":"Advancing the profession is a stated purpose; maximizing compensation is not."},"Standard A.9 permits use or disclosure of confidential client information for which two purposes?":{"Marketing and recruiting":"Marketing and recruiting are not A.9 confidentiality exceptions — only ordinary business and legal/compliance use.","Tax filing and social media":"Social media disclosure is never permitted; the exceptions are ordinary business and legal/compliance use.","Referral solicitation and advertising":"Advertising/solicitation are not confidentiality exceptions under A.9."},"Which correctly orders steps 4 through 6 of the Financial Planning Process?":{"Developing &rarr; Implementing &rarr; Presenting":"Presenting (Step 5) comes before Implementing (Step 6), not after.","Presenting &rarr; Developing &rarr; Implementing":"Developing (Step 4) precedes Presenting (Step 5); this reverses the first two.","Implementing &rarr; Developing &rarr; Presenting":"Implementing is Step 6, not first — order is Develop, Present, Implement."},"Which is a CORRECT use of the CFP&reg; marks?":{"John Doe, C.F.P.":"Periods are not permitted — the marks use no periods and require the &reg; symbol.","John Doe, a CFP":"The marks may not be used as a noun and must include the &reg; symbol.","John Doe, cfp":"Lowercase is incorrect — the marks require capital letters and the &reg; symbol."},"What is the basic FDIC insured amount, per depositor, per ownership category, per institution?":{"$100,000":"$100,000 was the old pre-2008 limit; the current FDIC limit is $250,000.","$500,000":"$500,000 is the SIPC securities limit, not the FDIC deposit limit of $250,000.","Unlimited":"FDIC coverage is capped at $250,000 per depositor, per ownership category, per institution — not unlimited."},"Which of the following is NOT covered by FDIC insurance?":{"A certificate of deposit (CD)":"A CD is a deposit product fully covered by FDIC insurance.","A savings account":"A savings account is a deposit account covered by FDIC insurance.","A money market deposit account":"A money market DEPOSIT account is FDIC-insured; a money market MUTUAL FUND (a security) is not."},"Which agency conducts monetary policy?":{"OCC":"The OCC charters and supervises national banks; it does not conduct monetary policy.","FDIC":"The FDIC insures bank deposits; monetary policy is the Federal Reserve's role.","NCUA":"The NCUA insures/regulates credit unions; it does not set monetary policy."},"A securities firm trades from its own account and charges clients the difference between the interdealer offer price and the price it charges. This compensation is a:":{"Commission":"A commission is charged by a broker/agent ARRANGING a trade, not a dealer trading from its own inventory.","Advisory fee":"An advisory fee compensates ongoing advice, not a principal trade spread.","Loaded sales charge":"A sales load applies to mutual fund purchases, not to a dealer's principal-trade markup."},"In the 7-step process, which step immediately follows &ldquo;Developing the Recommendations&rdquo;?":{"Implementing the Recommendations":"Implementing is Step 6 — it comes AFTER presenting, not immediately after developing.","Analyzing the Current Course":"Analyzing is Step 3 — it comes BEFORE developing, not after.","Monitoring Progress":"Monitoring is the final Step 7 — several steps too late."},"A planner discovers the client&rsquo;s data is incomplete and the client refuses to provide more. What are the planner&rsquo;s two options?":{"Estimate the data or proceed anyway":"Proceeding without a sound basis violates the duty of care — the planner cannot fabricate or assume missing data.","Report the client to the CFP Board":"The Board polices practitioners, not clients — refusing to share data is not a reportable offense.","Charge a higher fee or proceed":"Fees are unrelated to the data problem; proceeding without adequate information is not permitted."},"Which of the following is considered QUALITATIVE client data?":{"The client&rsquo;s tax returns":"Tax returns are hard numbers — objective/quantitative data.","The client&rsquo;s investment statements":"Account statements are measurable dollar figures — quantitative, not qualitative.","The client&rsquo;s liabilities":"Debts are dollar amounts — objective/quantitative data."},"Which documentation method is NOT approved for client records?":{"CRM software":"CRM systems are an approved, standard method for documenting client records.","Handwritten notes":"Handwritten notes are an accepted documentation method.","Emails":"Email correspondence is an approved way to document client interactions."},"A 52-year-old with growing net worth who has become more risk averse and newly concerned about disability is in which life-cycle phase?":{"Asset Accumulation":"Accumulation (~early career–45) has limited discretionary income and higher risk tolerance — not rising net worth and risk aversion.","Distribution / Gifting":"Distribution/gifting (~60+) centers on estate planning and capital preservation, not newly building net worth.","Retirement Drawdown":"Drawdown is a post-retirement spending phase, not a 52-year-old still growing assets."},"Which is the only disclosure that must be provided in WRITING for all engagements?":{"Material conflicts of interest":"Conflicts may be disclosed verbally OR in writing — writing is not mandatory for them.","Terms of engagement":"Terms of engagement need not be written for every engagement — the privacy policy is the writing-required item.","Fee schedule":"Fee disclosure can be made in a form other than a standalone written schedule for all engagements."},"Unless implementation is excluded from the scope of engagement, who is responsible for implementing the recommendations?":{"The client alone":"The client isn't left to implement alone — the planner carries implementation duty unless it's excluded from scope.","A third-party custodian":"A custodian may execute transactions, but responsibility for implementation rests with the planner.","The CFP Board":"The Board sets standards; it has no role in implementing a client's plan."},"Which usage of the marks is correct?":{"John Doe, C.F.P.":"Periods are incorrect — the marks use no periods (CFP, not C.F.P.).","John Doe, CFP":"Missing the required ® registration symbol.","John Doe is a cfp":"Lowercase and used as a noun — the marks must be capitalized adjectives with ®, never lowercase."},"A client buys $2,000 of furniture on a credit card this month. On the personal financial statements this is recorded as a:":{"Cash outflow this month":"No cash left the account yet — it's a cash outflow only when the card is actually paid.","Variable expense and an asset":"Charging on credit creates a liability, not an expense; furniture is a use asset but the entry is the debt owed.","Reduction of net worth equal to interest only":"The full $2,000 liability reduces net worth (offset by the asset), not just the interest."},"In the cash flow statement, which outflow category is listed FIRST?":{"Fixed outflows":"Fixed outflows come after savings — the 'pay yourself first' rule lists savings & investments first.","Taxes":"Taxes are an outflow but are not the first-listed category; savings/investments lead.","Variable outflows":"Variable outflows are discretionary and listed later, not first."},"Which income figure is used for the consumer (non-mortgage) debt ratio?":{"Gross monthly income":"Gross income is used for the housing and total-debt ratios — the consumer ratio uses net (after-tax) income.","Adjusted gross income":"AGI is a tax concept, not the basis for the consumer debt ratio.","Taxable income":"Taxable income is a tax figure — the consumer debt ratio uses net take-home pay."},"A client&rsquo;s monthly housing cost (PITI) is $2,520 and gross monthly income is $8,000. The front-end ratio is and it is:":{"31.5% — within the 28% guideline":"The math is right (31.5%) but the conclusion is wrong — 31.5% exceeds the 28% limit.","28% — exactly at the limit":"Wrong figure — $2,520/$8,000 is 31.5%, not 28%.","22.5% — within guideline":"Miscalculation — the ratio is 31.5%, not 22.5%."},"A single client who is the household&rsquo;s only wage earner should hold an emergency fund of:":{"1 month of expenses":"Far too little — a single/one-income earner needs 6 months of expenses.","3 months of expenses":"3 months fits a dual-income household; a sole wage earner should hold 6.","12 months of expenses":"12 months exceeds the standard guideline — 6 months is the benchmark for a single earner."},"Which is the MOST heavily weighted FICO component?":{"Amounts owed (30%)":"Amounts owed is 30% — the second-largest factor, not the most heavily weighted.","Length of credit history (15%)":"Length of history is only 15% — a minor factor versus payment history.","Credit mix (10%)":"Credit mix is the smallest factor at 10%."},"A client wants to pay off debt in the way that saves the most money overall. Recommend the:":{"Snowball method (smallest balance first)":"Snowball targets small balances for motivation, but paying low-rate debts first costs more interest overall.","Minimum payments on all":"Paying only minimums maximizes interest paid — the opposite of saving money.","Consolidation regardless of rate":"Consolidating without regard to rate can raise the effective rate and cost more, not less."},"Which is NOT covered by FDIC insurance?":{"A joint checking account":"A joint bank deposit account is FDIC-insured (with separate coverage per owner).","A traditional IRA held in CDs at the bank":"Bank CDs, including those in an IRA, are FDIC-insured deposits.","A revocable trust account":"Revocable trust deposit accounts are FDIC-insured (coverage per beneficiary)."},"A borrower with no down payment funds who is an active service member is best served by a:":{"FHA loan":"FHA still requires a down payment (~3.5%) and mortgage insurance — VA offers zero-down for service members.","Jumbo loan":"Jumbo loans exceed conforming limits and typically demand larger down payments and strong credit.","Interest-only loan":"Interest-only addresses payment structure, not the down-payment barrier, and isn't service-member specific."},"Net investment assets are $400,000 and net worth is $1,000,000. The net-investment-assets-to-net-worth ratio is and it is:":{"40% — above the 50% guideline":"The 40% figure is right but 40% is BELOW the ≥50% target, not above it.","50% — at the guideline":"Wrong figure — $400K/$1,000K is 40%, not 50%.","25% — below guideline":"Miscalculation — the ratio is 40%, not 25%."},"PMI is generally required when:":{"The loan term exceeds 15 years":"Loan term doesn't trigger PMI — a down payment under 20% does.","The borrower chooses a fixed rate":"Rate type is irrelevant to PMI — it's driven by loan-to-value.","The home is a primary residence":"Occupancy doesn't require PMI; a low down payment (LTV > 80%) does."},"A lease requires payments at the beginning of each month. Which calculator mode is correct?":{"END mode (ordinary annuity)":"END mode is for end-of-period payments (ordinary annuity) like a mortgage — a lease pays at the beginning.","Either mode gives the same answer":"The modes differ by one period of interest, so they give different answers.","Amortization mode":"Amortization is for splitting payments into principal/interest, not for setting payment timing."},"For two otherwise identical annuities, the future value of the annuity due will be:":{"Smaller than the ordinary annuity":"Backwards — the annuity due earns an extra period of interest, so its FV is larger, not smaller.","Equal to the ordinary annuity":"They aren't equal — the timing difference gives the annuity due a larger FV.","Impossible to determine":"It's fully determinable — the annuity due is always larger for identical terms."},"A project has a positive NPV at the investor&rsquo;s required rate. This means:":{"The actual return is below the discount rate":"That describes a NEGATIVE NPV — positive NPV means the return exceeds the discount rate.","NPV always equals IRR":"NPV and IRR are different measures; they aren't equal.","The project should be rejected":"Positive NPV means accept, not reject."},"When setting up a SERIAL payment problem, the desired goal amount is entered as:":{"The future inflated amount as FV":"Serial payments use the goal in today's dollars, not the inflated future amount.","PMT in today&rsquo;s dollars":"The goal is entered as FV, not PMT; the first payment is what's solved for.","PV at the nominal rate":"The goal is FV using the inflation-adjusted rate, not PV at the nominal rate."},"In the early years of a fixed mortgage, each payment consists of:":{"More principal than interest":"Reversed — early payments are mostly interest; principal dominates only in later years.","Equal principal and interest":"The split isn't equal early on — interest far outweighs principal at the start.","Interest only":"Standard amortizing mortgages always pay some principal — they aren't interest-only."},"On a financial calculator, money the investor pays out should be entered as:":{"A positive number":"Positive is for inflows (money received) — outflows are negative.","Zero":"Cash outflows have a real value and must be entered with a negative sign, not zero.","Either sign works":"Sign matters — the calculator's cash-flow logic requires outflows negative and inflows positive."},"Which is the LARGEST component of U.S. GDP?":{"Investment (I)":"Business investment is a smaller share — consumption (~two-thirds) is the largest component.","Government spending (G)":"Government spending is significant but well below consumption's roughly two-thirds share.","Net exports (NE)":"Net exports are the smallest (and often negative) component."},"To pursue EXPANSIONARY monetary policy, the Federal Reserve would:":{"Sell government securities":"Selling securities drains reserves — that's contractionary (tightening), the opposite of expansionary.","Raise the discount rate":"Raising the discount rate tightens credit — a contractionary move.","Raise reserve requirements":"Raising reserve requirements reduces lending capacity — contractionary, not expansionary."},"Which interest rate does the Federal Reserve control DIRECTLY?":{"The prime rate":"The prime rate is set by commercial banks, not the Fed.","The federal funds rate":"The Fed targets and influences the fed funds rate but doesn't set it directly — banks lend at it.","Mortgage rates":"Mortgage rates are set by the market, not directly by the Fed."},"Stock prices are classified as which type of economic indicator?":{"Lagging":"Lagging indicators (prime rate, CPI) turn after the economy — stocks move ahead of it.","Coincident":"Coincident indicators move with the economy; stocks anticipate it, making them leading.","Neutral":"There is no 'neutral' indicator class — stocks are a leading indicator."},"Which is a LAGGING economic indicator?":{"Housing starts":"Housing starts are a leading indicator — they anticipate economic activity.","New orders for durable goods":"New durable-goods orders are a classic leading indicator.","Stock prices":"Stock prices are a leading indicator, moving ahead of the economy."},"A recession is technically defined as real GDP declining for:":{"1 consecutive quarter":"One quarter isn't enough — a recession requires two consecutive quarters of GDP decline.","4 consecutive quarters":"Four quarters overstates it — the technical threshold is two consecutive quarters.","6 consecutive quarters":"Six quarters describes a depression, not a recession."},"Which debt is generally NOT dischargeable in bankruptcy?":{"Credit-card balances":"Unsecured credit-card debt is generally dischargeable in bankruptcy.","Medical bills":"Medical debt is unsecured and typically dischargeable.","Unsecured personal loans":"Unsecured personal loans are generally dischargeable."},"Under the Truth in Lending Act, a consumer&rsquo;s liability for unauthorized credit-card charges is capped at:":{"$0":"TILA caps liability at $50, not $0 (though card issuers often waive it, the legal cap is $50).","$500":"$500 is too high — the statutory cap is $50.","The full balance":"The consumer is never liable for the full balance on unauthorized charges — liability is capped at $50."},"A consumer must notify a creditor of a billing error within how many days under the Fair Credit Billing Act?":{"30 days":"30 days is the creditor's response window, not the 60-day consumer notification deadline.","90 days":"90 days is the resolution timeframe, not the notification deadline (60 days).","120 days":"Too long — the consumer must notify within 60 days of the statement."},"Identity theft should be reported to which agency?":{"The SEC":"The SEC regulates securities markets, not identity-theft reporting.","FINRA":"FINRA oversees broker-dealers — it doesn't handle consumer identity theft.","The Federal Reserve":"The Fed conducts monetary policy; identity theft is reported to the FTC."},"In a period of deflation, the historically preferred investment is:":{"Common stocks":"Stocks generally fall in deflation as prices and profits drop — they're avoided.","Gold":"Gold is an inflation hedge; in deflation cash and bonds are preferred, not gold.","Real estate":"Real estate values typically decline in deflation — not the preferred holding."},"Gasoline and prescription drugs, which see little change in quantity demanded when price rises, are examples of:":{"Elastic goods":"Elastic goods show large demand swings with price — necessities like these barely respond (inelastic).","Complementary goods":"Complementary describes goods used together (e.g., cars/gas), not price sensitivity.","Substitute goods":"Substitutes are interchangeable goods — that's not what low price-sensitivity means."},"In the 3-step education funding process, the FIRST step (&ldquo;Inflate&rdquo;) uses which calculator mode and rate?":{"BEG mode, inflation-adjusted rate":"That's Step 2 (Adjust) — Step 1 uses END mode with the raw education inflation rate.","END mode, investment return rate":"Step 1 inflates cost using the education INFLATION rate, not the investment return.","BEG mode, investment return rate":"Wrong on both — Step 1 is END mode with the education inflation rate."},"The second step of education funding (the &ldquo;Adjust&rdquo; lump-sum calculation) is done in:":{"END mode with the investment return rate":"Step 2 is the PV of an annuity due — BEG mode with the inflation-adjusted rate, not END/return rate.","BEG mode with the nominal return":"Right mode, wrong rate — use the inflation-adjusted rate, not the nominal return.","END mode with education inflation":"END mode and education inflation describe Step 1 (Inflate), not Step 2."},"Which asset is EXCLUDED when calculating the Student Aid Index (SAI)?":{"A parent-owned 529 plan":"A parent-owned 529 is counted as a parent asset (~5.64%), not excluded.","A student&rsquo;s UTMA account":"A student's UTMA is counted, and heavily — at 20% as a student asset.","A taxable brokerage account":"Taxable brokerage assets are counted in the SAI."},"A student&rsquo;s UTMA custodial account is assessed for SAI at what rate?":{"5.64%":"5.64% is the PARENT asset rate — student assets are assessed higher, at 20%.","50%":"50% applies to student INCOME above the allowance, not student assets.","0%":"Student custodial assets are counted, not excluded — at 20%."},"Which education credit is partially REFUNDABLE?":{"Lifetime Learning Credit":"The LLC is nonrefundable — only the AOTC is partially (40%) refundable.","Both are fully refundable":"Neither is fully refundable — the AOTC is only 40% refundable and the LLC is nonrefundable.","Neither is refundable":"The AOTC is 40% refundable (up to $1,000), so this is wrong."},"A graduate student taking one course wants a tax credit. Which applies?":{"AOTC only":"The AOTC is limited to the first four years of a degree — it doesn't cover grad courses.","Both equally":"The AOTC excludes grad students, so only the LLC applies.","Neither — grad students are ineligible":"Grad students ARE eligible — the LLC has no degree or year limit."},"The maximum annual contribution to a Coverdell ESA per beneficiary is:":{"$7,000":"$7,000 is the 2024 IRA limit, not the Coverdell — the ESA cap is $2,000.","$10,000":"$10,000 has no bearing on Coverdells (it's near the 529 K-12 tuition limit) — the ESA cap is $2,000.","$19,000":"$19,000 is the 2025 gift-tax annual exclusion, not the Coverdell limit ($2,000)."},"Series EE/I bond interest is excludable for education only when used for:":{"Tuition, fees, room, and board":"Room and board don't qualify — the bond exclusion covers higher-ed tuition and fees only.","Any K-12 or college expense":"The exclusion is limited to HIGHER-ed tuition/fees, not K-12 or any expense.","Books and supplies":"Books and supplies aren't qualifying expenses for the bond exclusion — only tuition and fees."},"A child&rsquo;s unearned investment income above the threshold is taxed at:":{"The child&rsquo;s rate":"Only unearned income BELOW the threshold uses the child's rate — above it, the kiddie tax uses the parent's rate.","A flat 10%":"There's no flat 10% — excess unearned income is taxed at the parent's marginal rate.","0% always":"Excess unearned income is taxable at the parent's rate, not zero."},"Using 5-year superfunding in 2025, a single donor may front-load a 529 with up to:":{"$19,000":"$19,000 is a single year's exclusion — superfunding front-loads five years at once.","$38,000":"$38,000 is a married couple's one-year gift — single-donor 5-year superfunding is $95,000.","$190,000":"$190,000 would be a married couple's 5-year superfund — a single donor is capped at $95,000."},"A federal direct student loan goes into default after how many days of delinquency?":{"90 days":"90 days is early delinquency, not default — federal loans default at 270 days.","180 days":"180 days is still short of default; federal direct loans default at 270 days.","365 days":"One year is beyond the mark — default occurs at 270 days delinquent."},"Grandparent-owned 529 distributions now count toward the SAI as:":{"Student income at 50%":"That was the OLD FAFSA rule — under the simplified SAI, grandparent 529 distributions count at 0%.","A parent asset at 5.64%":"Grandparent-owned 529s aren't parent assets — distributions now count at 0%.","A student asset at 20%":"20% is the student-asset rate, but grandparent 529 distributions are no longer counted at all."},"If the practitioner is unable to obtain sufficient and relevant quantitative information and documents to form a basis for recommendations, the practitioner must do which of these? I. Restrict the scope of the engagement. II. Terminate the engagement.":{"I only":"Restricting scope is one valid option, but terminating is equally allowed — it's either/or, not I alone.","II only":"Terminating is valid, but restricting the scope is equally permitted — it's either/or, not II alone.","Neither I nor II":"Both are the prescribed responses — the practitioner must choose one, not neither."},"Identify the CORRECT statements regarding financial strengths and weaknesses. I. Inadequate retirement savings is considered a financial weakness. II. Very general financial goals are considered a financial strength. III. Determining financial strengths and weaknesses is an objective process. IV. The lack of a valid will is considered a financial weakness if a will is necessary to protect the interest of heirs.":{"I only":"Statement IV (lack of a needed will is a weakness) is also correct, so I only is incomplete.","III and IV":"III is false — assessing strengths/weaknesses is a SUBJECTIVE process, not objective.","I, II, III, and IV 19":"II is false (vague goals are a weakness, not a strength) and III is false (it's subjective)."},"Sophia, a CFP® professional, is meeting with Eric and Emily to review the two recommendations included in their financial plan. The first recommendation discussed by Sophia was for Eric and Emily to “purchase term life insurance because it is inexpensive.” When asked":{"II and III":"Omits statement I and includes III — the correct set is I, II, and IV per the analysis.","III and IV":"Excludes the applicable statements I and II; the correct combination is I, II, and IV.","I, II, III, and IV":"Overinclusive — statement III does not apply; the correct set is I, II, and IV."},"Analyze this scenario. Bari, a CFP® professional, is providing Financial Advice to her client, Jamal. After considering Jamal’s goals, family medical history, tax situation, and financial resources, she develops a financial plan that includes a recommendation for Jamal to purchase disability income insurance. Which statement regarding implementation responsibilities is CORRECT? I. Bari is not responsible for implementing this planning recommendation because it only involves the purchase of a single product. II. Bari must explain to Jamal the responsibilities she has in implementing the recommendations and the responsibilities that Jamal and any third party may have with respect to implementation. III. If Bari and Jamal have not excluded implementation responsibilities from the Engagement, Bari must recommend one or more disability insurance policies to Jamal and help him select a policy that will meet his needs. IV. If Bari and Jamal have not excluded implementation responsibilities from the Engagement, Bari must identify and analyze policies designed to implement the recommendations and must consider advantages and disadvantages of the disability product relative to reasonably available alternatives.":{"I and II":"Statement I is false — a single-product recommendation doesn't relieve Bari of implementation duty; III and IV also apply.","II and III":"Incomplete — statement IV (analyzing alternatives) is also required; the correct set is II, III, and IV.","I, II, III, and IV 21":"Statement I is false — Bari IS responsible for implementation unless it's excluded from scope."},"Kevin and Gina, ages 45 and 43, respectively, have engaged Mei, a CFP® professional, to help them develop their financial plan. All of the following should be considered when developing Kevin and Gina’s financial plan except":{"their current position in the life cycle.":"Life-cycle positioning IS a proper planning consideration — the question asks for the one that is not.","their values, goals, and objectives.":"Values, goals, and objectives are central to plan development — they should be considered.","their financial position.":"Their financial position is core input for the plan — it should be considered."},"A person in the conservation or protection phase of the financial life cycle is likely to have which of the following goals?":{"Short-term goals, such as saving for a down payment on a home":"Short-term, cost-of-living goals characterize the ACCUMULATION phase, not conservation/protection.","Long-term goals, such as education planning and preservation of capital":"Capital preservation and estate concerns belong to the DISTRIBUTION/gifting phase, not conservation.","Short-term goals, such as protection and maintenance of current lifestyle":"The conservation phase shifts to LONG-term goals like retirement, not short-term lifestyle maintenance."},"Which of the following uses of the CFP® certification marks is CORRECT? I. John Doe, C.F.P. II. John Doe, a CFP III. John Doe, CERTIFIED FINANCIAL PLANNER® IV. John Doe, CFP®":{"IV only":"Too narrow. Statement III (CERTIFIED FINANCIAL PLANNER® with the ® mark) is also correct, so IV alone is incomplete.","I and III":"Statement I is wrong: the marks may not be shown with periods (C.F.P.). Correct form is CFP®.","II and IV":"Statement II is wrong: the marks are adjectives, not nouns; you cannot write 'a CFP.' Only IV is valid here."},"Which of the following statements regarding financial transparency is CORRECT?":{"Partners should be transparent regarding their finances only if they have joint accounts.":"Wrong condition. Transparency is needed even with separate accounts, not just joint ones.","It is important that partners always share financial goals and values.":"Too absolute. Partners need to share OR respect differing goals with agreement, not necessarily hold identical ones.","If partners appear uncomfortable discussing the details of their finances, the planner should, at the very least, get an overview of them.":"An overview is insufficient; sound planning requires full details of income, spending, assets, and liabilities."},"Your client, Kamari, needs assistance preparing his statement of financial position and cash flow statement. He has an annual salary of $200,000 and pays $1,200 monthly in alimony to his ex-spouse, Diana. Kamari owns a condo valued at $300,000, which currently has an outstanding mortgage balance of $130,000. He pays annual property taxes of $4,000, and a monthly condo insurance premium of $250. All of the following statements are correct except":{"Kamari’s salary would be considered a cash inflow on his cash flow statement.":"This statement is correct (salary is an inflow), so it is not the exception the question seeks.","the alimony Kamari pays would be a cash inflow on Diana’s cash flow statement.":"Correct statement: alimony received is an inflow to Diana, so it is not the exception.","the condo insurance payments would be a fixed outflow on Kamari’s cash flow statement.":"Correct statement: insurance premiums are fixed outflows, so it is not the exception."},"Analyze the following scenario. Caitlin has the following assets: a checking account of $2,500, a savings account of $5,000, and a money market mutual fund of $3,500. She also has mutual fund investments totaling $125,000. She has a personal balloon note liability of $25,000 coming due within the next year. Does Caitlin’s current ratio represent a potential problem with respect to her financial situation?":{"No, her current investments are adequate to cover her current liabilities.":"The $125,000 mutual funds are NOT current assets; only the $11,000 liquid accounts count, so current assets fall short.","No, her current ratio is 1.44, which is very favorable.":"Miscalculated by wrongly including the $125,000 investments as current assets. True current assets are $11,000.","Yes, her current ratio is negative. 89":"A ratio of current assets to liabilities cannot be negative; it is 0.44, a positive but inadequate figure."},"The following is Bryan and Hannah Smith’s statement of cash flows. Statement of Cash Flows Bryan and Hannah Smith For the Current Year Cash inflows Salary—Bryan $54,000 Salary—Hannah 45,000 Interest—investments 1,000 Total inflows $100,000 Cash outflows $100,000 Mortgage payment (principal and interest) $16,000 Homeowners insurance 2,800 Utilities 5,400 Property taxes 1,000 Cell phones 2,400 Automobile loan payment 5,000 Auto maintenance 2,000 Child care 5,000 Auto fuel/commuting expenses 5,000 Food 7,000 Credit card payments 3,600 Education loan payments 2,000 Entertainment 1,000 FICA, federal, and state withholding taxes 27,700 Other expenses 4,100 Total outflows 90,000 Net cash flow (surplus) $10,000 Calculate the Smiths’ consumer debt and determine their financial status.":{"Borderline—near the maximum considered financially prudent":"The consumer debt ratio is 14.66%, well under the 20% limit, not borderline.","Poor—debt is much higher than standard guidelines":"Consumer debt ($10,600 of auto, cards, education loans) is 14.66% of income, below the 20% ceiling, not poor.","Cannot be determined 90":"It can be determined: sum consumer debt payments and divide by income to get 14.66%."},"Which of the following can be monitored and evaluated through the use of a budget? I. Income II. Net worth III. Expenses IV. Spending patterns":{"I and III":"Incomplete; a budget also tracks spending patterns (IV), not just income and expenses.","II and IV":"Includes net worth (II), which is measured by the statement of financial position, not a budget.","I, II, and III":"Wrongly includes net worth (II); net worth comes from the balance sheet, not the budget."},"Tim and Gina are working with you, their financial planner, to develop a budget. As part of the process, you ask them to list their discretionary and nondiscretionary expenses. Which of the following should Tim and Gina consider to be nondiscretionary cash outflows for planning purposes? I. Utility bills II. Loan payments III. Travel and entertainment expenses IV. Medical and dental insurance premiums":{"I and II":"Incomplete; medical/dental insurance premiums (IV) are also nondiscretionary.","II and IV":"Omits utility bills (I), which are also nondiscretionary necessities.","I, II, III, and IV":"Includes travel and entertainment (III), which are discretionary, not nondiscretionary."},"After meeting with you, Janesh and Katie understand the need for an emergency fund. Janesh is a mechanic, and Katie volunteers at a local hospital. They ask you how much they should have in this fund. Which of the following is your best response?":{"An amount equal to 1 month of expenses":"Far too low; the standard rule of thumb is 3 to 6 months, not one.","An amount equal to 3 months of expenses":"Three months fits dual-income couples; here only Janesh earns income, so six months is needed.","An amount equal to 12 months of expenses 97":"Twelve months exceeds the standard guideline; six months suffices for a single-earner household."},"Miguel and Michelle, both age 38, would like to create an emergency fund for major unexpected expenses. Which of the following accounts are appropriate for this fund? I. Stocks and bonds II. Savings account III. Traditional IRA account IV. Certificate of deposit (CD) with a three-month maturity":{"I and II":"Stocks and bonds (I) risk principal loss and are not liquid enough for an emergency fund.","III and IV":"A traditional IRA (III) triggers taxes and a 10% penalty before 59½, making it unsuitable for emergencies.","II, III, and IV":"Includes the traditional IRA (III), which is illiquid and penalty-exposed for these age-38 clients."},"You have advised your client, Bette, that she needs to increase her savings. What might you recommend as good savings strategies? I. Plant a large vegetable and herb garden. II. Use an overdraft feature on her debit card. III. Consider a health insurance plan with a lower deductible.":{"I and II":"The overdraft feature (II) encourages spending money she lacks, so it undermines saving.","II and III":"Both are poor strategies: overdraft (II) tempts overspending and a lower deductible (III) raises premiums.","I, II, and III":"II (overdraft) and III (lower deductible, higher premiums) both hurt savings; only I helps."},"Over the years, Gabriel has made timely payments on three of his credit card accounts, all which have balances near the available credit limits. He also paid off a fourth credit card account, which he had for 20 years, and immediately closed it. Which of the following statements regarding Gabriel’s credit score is CORRECT? I. By immediately closing his long-standing account when it was paid off, Gabriel likely increased his credit score. II. Having three credit card account balances near their available credit limits adversely affects Gabriel’s credit score.":{"I only":"Closing a 20-year account shortens credit history and raises utilization, lowering the score, not raising it.","Both I and II":"Statement I is false: closing the long-standing account likely decreased his score.","Neither I nor II":"Statement II is true: high utilization near credit limits hurts the score."},"What is the best reason that your client, Jennie, should rent an apartment rather than purchase a home?":{"She does not want to do yard work.":"A weak reason; many condos and homes have no yard duties, so this alone doesn't favor renting.","She does not want to pay real property taxes.":"Property taxes are income-tax deductible if she itemizes, offsetting the cost, so this is a weak rationale.","She does not want to purchase homeowners insurance. 110":"Renters still need an HO-4 policy for contents and liability, so avoiding insurance is not a valid reason."},"Which mortgage is generally available for lower-income households who would be unlikely to secure a conventional home loan?":{"Reverse mortgage":"A reverse mortgage is for homeowners 62+ tapping equity, not for lower-income buyers financing a purchase.","Balloon mortgage":"A balloon loan features a large final payment; it targets loan structure, not low-income accessibility.","Interest-only mortgage":"Interest-only loans defer principal; they don't address qualification barriers for lower-income buyers."},"Oscar has just joined the faculty of a local college as an associate professor. He and his spouse, Kathy, would like to purchase a new home near the college. They are meeting with their loan officer to determine which mortgage would best suit their needs. Kathy is a stay-at-home mom raising the couple’s four school-age children; she sometimes provides bookkeeping services for her friends. Oscar and Kathy, both age 45, want a mortgage that offers the lowest monthly payment with a fixed interest rate. Based on this information, which mortgage would be the most appropriate choice?":{"Veterans Administration (VA) mortgage":"Nothing indicates military service, so they cannot qualify for a VA loan.","Reverse mortgage":"Reverse mortgages require age 62+; at 45 they are ineligible, and it doesn't finance a purchase.","15-year conventional mortgage":"A 15-year loan has a higher monthly payment than a 30-year; they want the lowest payment."},"Which of the following statements regarding home equity lines of credit (HELOCs) is CORRECT? I. Borrowers repay the loan with equal monthly payments over a fixed term. II. Clients are given a set amount of credit from which they can draw from as funds are needed. III. Borrowers make payments only on the amount they actually borrow, not the full amount available. IV. HELOCs use the current equity in the homeowner’s primary residence to provide money for home improvements and other purposes.":{"II and IV":"Incomplete; III (paying only on amounts drawn) is also a correct HELOC feature.","III and IV":"Omits II, the revolving credit line feature that defines a HELOC.","I, II, and III":"Includes I, which describes a fixed-term home equity LOAN, not a revolving HELOC."},"Which of the following actions would be appropriate for a homeowner who has a goal to retire debt and reduce the amount of total interest due over the life of his mortgage loan? I. Replace a 30-year fixed-rate loan with a 15-year fixed-rate loan. II. Replace a 15-year fixed-rate loan with a 30-year fixed-rate loan. III. Replace a fixed-rate loan with a lower interest fixed-rate loan of the same term.":{"I only":"Incomplete; III (refinancing to a lower rate, same term) also reduces total interest.","II and III":"Includes II, which extends the term to 30 years and greatly increases total interest paid.","I, II, and III 117":"Includes II, which lengthens repayment and adds interest, contradicting the goal."},"Which of the following statements regarding banks is CORRECT? I. The Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) makes monetary policy. II. The FDIC charters, supervises, and regulates national banks and federal branches of foreign banks located in the United States.":{"I only":"Statement I is false: the Federal Reserve Board, not the OCC, makes monetary policy.","II only":"Statement II is false: the OCC (not the FDIC) charters and regulates national banks; the FDIC insures deposits.","Both I and II":"Both are false: the Fed makes monetary policy and the OCC charters national banks."},"Sarah, a CFP® professional, completed a life insurance application for her client, Chase. When Chase called her two weeks later about the status of the application, Sarah realized she had not submitted it to the insurance company. Which of the following ethical principles has Sarah violated?":{"Standard A.2 (Integrity)":"Integrity concerns honesty and candor; the failure here was delay/thoroughness, which is Diligence.","Standard A.6 (Sound and Objective Professional Judgement)":"This addresses objectivity free of bias, not the timely, thorough delivery of services.","Standard A.7 (Professionalism)":"Professionalism concerns courteous, dignified conduct; the lapse was untimely service, i.e., Diligence."},"Following consideration of a Respondent’s petition, the Hearing Panel must include which of the following with its recommendation? I. Factual findings II. Mitigating factors III. Aggravating factors IV. Relevant Anonymous Case Histories":{"I only":"Too narrow; the recommendation must also include mitigating/aggravating factors and relevant Anonymous Case Histories.","I and IV":"Omits mitigating (II) and aggravating (III) factors, which must be included.","II and III":"Omits factual findings (I) and relevant Anonymous Case Histories (IV), which are also required."},"Movement through the phases of the business cycle is initiated by shifts in aggregate demand that create fluctuations in gross domestic product (GDP). Which combination of the following statements would be the most significant contributor to the upward shift in aggregate demand shown in the graph? (Note: This is a previously released CFP® Certification Exam question.) Price D1 D0 Quantity I. Increase in demand for capital goods II. Increase in interest rates III. Increase in disposable income IV. Increase in savings":{"I, II, and III":"Includes II: rising interest rates dampen borrowing and spending, decreasing aggregate demand.","I, III, and IV":"Includes IV: more savings means less current spending, which reduces aggregate demand.","II and IV E. III and IV":"Both II (higher rates) and IV (more savings) shift aggregate demand down, not up."},"Identify the inelastic good(s). I. Stereo equipment II. Prescription medication III. Fine art IV. Electricity":{"I only":"Stereo equipment is a discretionary luxury with elastic demand, not inelastic.","I and II":"Includes stereo equipment (I), a luxury with elastic demand; only necessities like medication are inelastic.","I and III":"Both stereo equipment (I) and fine art (III) are luxuries with price-sensitive, elastic demand."},"The economy is approaching full employment and inflation is rising rapidly. What would be the prudent fiscal policy to enact under this scenario?":{"Raise taxes and raise the discount rate.":"The discount rate is a monetary tool of the Fed, not fiscal policy; the question asks for fiscal action.","Lower taxes and increase government spending.":"This is expansionary policy, which would worsen an overheating, inflationary economy.","Lower taxes and lower the discount rate.":"Expansionary and mixes in a monetary tool (discount rate); wrong direction for fighting inflation."},"The federal funds rate will tend to move upward under which of the following conditions? (Note: This is a previously released CFP® Certification Exam question.)":{"The Federal Reserve is buying government securities.":"Open-market purchases add reserves and money supply, pushing the fed funds rate down, not up.","The Federal Reserve lowers the discount rate.":"A lower discount rate signals easing and tends to pull the fed funds rate down.","A few banks have excess reserve deficiencies, and the rest have ample excess reserves.":"Ample overall reserves means high supply of lendable funds, pushing the rate down."},"Select the strategy the Fed would implement if it wanted to contract the overall economy.":{"Buy U.S. government securities in the open market.":"Buying securities injects reserves and expands the money supply, an expansionary (not contractionary) move.","Lower the discount rate.":"Lowering the discount rate encourages borrowing and expands money supply, the opposite of contraction.","Target the federal funds rate in a lower range than previously.":"A lower fed funds target is expansionary, stimulating rather than contracting the economy."},"The economy is experiencing an increase in housing starts and a decline in the unemployment rate. Based on this economic activity, identify the phase of the business cycle.":{"Peak":"At a peak, housing starts would be topping out and beginning to decline, not increasing.","Trough":"A trough is the bottom with high unemployment; here unemployment is falling and activity is rising.","Contraction":"Contraction features falling output and rising unemployment, opposite of the rising activity described."},"Identify the economic indicators that can best be described as preceding or leading to a change in the business cycle.":{"Unemployment rate and bond yields":"The unemployment rate is a lagging indicator, so this pair is not purely leading.","Prime rate of interest and industrial production":"Prime rate and industrial production are lagging/coincident indicators, not leading.","Amount of corporate profits and level of personal income":"Corporate profits and personal income are coincident/lagging, not leading indicators."},"Select the item that is NOT generally dischargeable by a debtor in a bankruptcy filing.":{"Veterans’ benefits":"The question asks about non-dischargeable obligations; veterans' benefits are protected assets, not a debt owed.","Equity in a personal residence":"Home equity is a protected asset (homestead), not an obligation being discharged.","A specified amount in a personal automobile":"Auto equity up to an exemption is a protected asset, not a non-dischargeable debt."},"All of the following are identity theft protection measures except":{"refusing to provide your Social Security number to internet solicitors.":"This IS a valid protective measure, so it is not the exception the question seeks.","regularly inspecting credit reports and reviewing billing statements.":"This is a sound protective measure, so it is not the exception.","closing any accounts you believe may have been established in error.":"Closing fraudulent accounts is a proper protective step, so it is not the exception."},"Which of these demonstrates fair information practices regulating the use of personal information? I. Written consent from an individual must be given before personal":{"I only":"Incomplete; both statements are correct, not just statement I.","II only":"Incomplete; statement I is also correct, so 'both' is the right answer.","Neither I nor II Match each description with the correct fair credit reporting law. Use each letter once. a. Requires credit bureau reports to 15. ___ Fair Credit Billing Act include accurate, relevant, and":"Wrong; both statements are correct, so 'neither' is incorrect."},"Scott and Barbara have come to you for advice on financing their daughter’s fast-approaching college undergraduate education. They have failed to save enough money to finance their daughter’s education but currently have an annual income in excess of $150,000. Unfortunately, they spend as much as they earn. Select a suitable option for the Johnsons if there is a need to obtain education funds.":{"Subsidized Stafford loan":"Subsidized Stafford loans are need-based; their $150,000+ income disqualifies them.","Pell Grant":"Pell Grants are need-based grants for low-income students; their high income rules it out.","Federal Work-Study Program":"Work-study is need-based aid; their income level makes them ineligible."},"Assume that an adult client is currently in a low marginal income tax bracket and does not anticipate major income increases in the future. He is interested in a low-risk investment purchased in his name that may provide favorable income tax treatment when used for the higher education tuition expenses of his only child. He is also not interested in incurring the expense of establishing a trust when saving for his child’s":{"An UGMA account":"An UGMA is an irrevocable gift owned by the child, not purchased in the client's name, and risks kiddie-tax treatment.","A passbook savings account in the child’s name":"Held in the child's name (not the client's) and offers no favorable education-tax treatment.","Municipal bonds purchased in the name of the client":"Muni interest is already tax-exempt, so the low-bracket client gains little, and there's no special education benefit."},"Erin’s grandmother recently established a CESA on behalf of Erin, age five, and contributed $2,000 to the account. Now, Erin’s father wants to contribute an additional $2,000 to the account in this same year. Is this permitted?":{"Yes, because both the grandmother and father are family members of Erin.":"The $2,000 cap is per beneficiary, not per donor; family status is irrelevant to the limit.","No, only Erin’s father is allowed to establish a CESA.":"Any donor may establish or contribute to a CESA; the donor need not be a parent or even a family member.","Yes, up to $2,000 per donor can be contributed on Erin’s behalf.":"The $2,000 limit is per beneficiary total, not per donor, so a second $2,000 exceeds it."},"Identify a difference between a Section 529 plan and a Coverdell ESA (CESA).":{"The account balance in a Section 529 plan must be distributed to the child upon reaching age 30.":"That age-30 distribution rule applies to Coverdell ESAs, not Section 529 plans.","The CESA may not be used for higher education expenses.":"False; a CESA can be used for qualified higher education expenses.","Contributions made to a CESA and not used for qualified education expenses may be reclaimed. 293":"Reversed; the contributor can reclaim unused 529 funds, whereas a CESA generally cannot be reclaimed."},"George and Betty wish to establish a Section 529 college savings plan for both of their grandchildren. In 2025, what is the maximum amount that they can contribute without making a taxable gift for federal gift tax purposes?":{"$95,000":"This is five-year front-loading for one donor to one beneficiary ($19,000 x 5); there are two donors and two grandchildren.","$190,000":"This covers one beneficiary funded by both spouses ($95,000 x 2); with two grandchildren the total doubles.","$280,000":"Doesn't match the five-year gift-tax math; two spouses x two grandchildren x $95,000 equals $380,000."},"Amanda is a half-time sophomore at ABC University. Tuition payable for 202X was $3,000. Amanda is claimed as a dependent on her parents’ 202X income tax return and her parents have reported a modified AGI of $150,000 for that year. Neither Amanda nor her parents are claiming any other education benefit for 202X. Which of the following statements regarding the possible applicability of the American Opportunity Tax Credit is CORRECT?":{"Amanda’s parents will be able to claim $2,000 of the American Opportunity Tax Credit on their 202X income tax return.":"Ignores the 25% credit on the next $1,000; the AOTC on $3,000 tuition is $2,000 + $250 = $2,250.","Amanda will be able to claim $3,000 of the American Opportunity Tax Credit on her 202X income tax return.":"A claimed dependent cannot take the credit; the parents do, and the credit isn't simply equal to tuition.","Neither Amanda nor her parents are able to take advantage of the American Opportunity Tax Credit for income tax purposes.":"The parents' MAGI is below the phaseout, so they can claim the credit."},"According to the Internal Revenue Code, which of the following statements regarding education assistance programs is CORRECT? I. An employer may provide an unlimited amount of educational assistance to an employee, assuming the education is job related. II. An employer may provide up to $7,250 of non-job-related educational assistance to an employee during any one year as a tax-free employee benefit.":{"II only":"The Section 127 limit is $5,250, not $7,250, so statement II is wrong.","Both I and II":"Statement II is wrong: the tax-free limit for non-job-related assistance is $5,250, not $7,250.","Neither I nor II 301":"Statement I is correct: job-related education assistance can be unlimited and tax-free."},"On the statement of financial position, net worth equals:":{"Assets + Liabilities":"Confuses the accounting identity; assets equal liabilities PLUS net worth, so you subtract liabilities.","Income &minus; Expenses":"That is net cash flow from the cash flow statement, not net worth from the balance sheet.","Liabilities &minus; Assets":"Reversed; subtracting assets from liabilities gives a negative of net worth. It is Assets minus Liabilities."},"At what value are assets reported on a personal statement of financial position?":{"Original purchase cost":"That is historical cost/basis; personal balance sheets use current fair market value.","FMV net of any early-withdrawal penalties":"FMV is used and penalties are ignored; you do not net them out.","Tax basis":"Tax basis is used for gain/loss calculations, not for reporting asset value on the statement of financial position."},"How is a mortgage reported on the statement of financial position?":{"At its original loan amount":"The original amount ignores principal already repaid; report the current outstanding balance.","At the monthly payment amount":"The payment is a cash-flow item, not the balance-sheet liability value.","At principal plus all remaining interest":"Future interest is not yet owed as a liability; only the outstanding principal is listed."},"A client's monthly PITI is $2,000 and gross monthly income is $8,000. The housing (front-end) ratio is and:":{"25%, exceeds the 28% benchmark":"25% is within, not above, the 28% front-end benchmark.","16.7%, uses net income":"The ratio uses gross income; $2,000/$8,000 = 25%, not 16.7%.","40%, exceeds the benchmark":"Miscalculated; $2,000/$8,000 is 25%, and it does not exceed 28%."},"The total (back-end) debt ratio benchmark is and is measured against income.":{"28%; net":"28% is the front-end (housing) benchmark, and the debt ratios use gross, not net, income.","20%; net":"20% net is the consumer-debt benchmark, not the total back-end ratio.","43%; gross":"43% is a qualified-mortgage lending threshold, not the standard 36% back-end planning benchmark."},"The consumer (non-mortgage) debt ratio benchmark of 20% is measured against which income figure?":{"Gross monthly income":"The housing and total-debt ratios use gross; the consumer-debt ratio uses net monthly income.","Adjusted gross income":"AGI is a tax concept; the consumer-debt ratio is measured against net monthly income.","Taxable income":"Taxable income is a tax figure; the 20% consumer-debt benchmark uses net monthly income."},"For which client is a 6-month emergency fund most appropriate?":{"Married couple, both employed at stable jobs":"Dual stable incomes support a 3-month fund; six months is for single-earner situations.","Married couple with a large second income available":"A second income cushions job loss, so three months suffices, not six.","Dual-income household with substantial liquid assets":"Two incomes plus ample liquidity call for three months, not six."},"The net-investment-assets-to-net-worth ratio is generally considered adequate when it is at least:":{"10%":"Too low; the target is at least 50%, though younger clients may start near 20% or less.","20%":"20% is typical for younger clients, not the 'adequate' benchmark, which is at least 50%.","100%":"Overstated; the ratio need only reach at least 50% and rise toward retirement, not 100%."},"Regarding the recommended savings ratio of at least 10% of gross income, employer qualified-plan contributions:":{"Do not count at all":"Employer qualified-plan contributions DO count toward the 10% savings ratio.","Count only if vested":"Vesting is irrelevant here; employer contributions count toward the savings ratio regardless.","Are counted as income, not savings":"Employer plan contributions are treated as savings, not as current income, for this ratio."},"A client paying off the smallest balances first to build motivation is using the:":{"Avalanche technique":"The avalanche targets the highest-interest debt first to minimize interest, not the smallest balance.","Refinancing technique":"Refinancing replaces a loan with better terms; it is not a debt-payoff ordering method.","Amortization technique":"Amortization is the scheduled paydown of a loan, not a motivational payoff strategy."},"Which factor carries the greatest weight in a FICO score?":{"Length of credit history (15%)":"At 15% it is a minor factor; payment history (35%) carries the greatest weight.","Amounts owed / utilization (30%)":"At 30% it is the second-largest factor, behind payment history at 35%.","New credit (10%)":"At only 10%, new credit is among the smallest FICO factors."},"One mortgage point equals:":{"1% of the home's value":"A point is based on the loan amount borrowed, not the home's value or price.","$1,000 regardless of loan size":"A point is 1% of the loan, so its dollar amount varies with loan size, not a flat $1,000.","1% of the monthly payment":"A point is 1% of the amount borrowed, not of the monthly payment."},"Which is true of a VA loan?":{"Requires at least 3.5% down":"The 3.5% minimum down payment applies to FHA loans; VA loans allow zero down.","Requires PMI when down payment is under 20%":"VA loans never require PMI; they charge a funding fee instead.","Is only available for jumbo amounts":"VA loans are for eligible veterans and are not restricted to jumbo loan amounts."},"What is the minimum age to obtain a reverse (home equity conversion) mortgage?":{"55":"Too young; the HECM minimum is age 62.","65":"The minimum is 62, not 65.","59&frac12;":"59½ is the retirement-account penalty age, not the reverse-mortgage minimum of 62."},"A 70-year-old retiree making lifetime gifts to heirs and executing estate strategies is primarily in which financial life-cycle phase?":{"Asset accumulation":"Accumulation is the early wealth-building phase, not the retiree gifting/estate stage.","Conservation/protection":"The conservation phase focuses on protecting and growing assets pre-retirement, before active gifting.","Liquidation":"Liquidation is not a standard named life-cycle phase; retirement gifting is the distribution/gifting phase."},"Lease payments and education/retirement funding contributions made at the beginning of each period are an example of a(n):":{"Ordinary annuity":"Ordinary annuities pay at period-END (e.g., mortgages); beginning-of-period payments are an annuity due.","Perpetuity":"A perpetuity pays forever with no end; these are finite, timing-specific payments.","Lump sum":"A lump sum is a single payment, not a series of beginning-of-period payments."},"Compared with simply subtracting inflation from the rate of return, the correctly computed inflation-adjusted (real) rate is:":{"Always slightly higher":"The correct real rate from the Fisher formula is slightly LOWER than return minus inflation, not higher.","Exactly equal":"Only approximately equal; the compounding in the true formula makes it slightly lower.","Unrelated":"They are closely related; the correct method just yields a slightly lower figure."},"If an investment has a negative net present value (NPV), then its return is:":{"Greater than the discount rate":"A return above the discount rate produces a POSITIVE NPV, not a negative one.","Equal to the discount rate":"When return equals the discount rate, NPV is zero, not negative.","Equal to zero":"A zero return is unrelated to NPV sign; negative NPV means return is below the discount rate."},"Compared with a level (fixed) annuity payment funding the same goal, the FIRST serial payment is:":{"Larger than the fixed payment":"Reversed; serial payments start smaller and grow, so the first is smaller, not larger.","Identical to the fixed payment":"Serial payments rise with inflation, so they are not equal to the level payment.","Zero":"The first serial payment is a real, positive amount, just smaller than the fixed one, not zero."},"A client parks on a steep hill without setting the parking brake. This best illustrates a:":{"Risk":"Risk is the uncertainty or potential for loss itself, not the condition that raises the chance of one. Here the failure to brake is what increases the odds.","Peril":"A peril is the actual cause of loss (the car rolling away). The unbraked parking is only the condition that makes that loss more likely.","Loss":"A loss is the realized harm/damage. No damage has occurred yet; this describes only a condition that increases loss likelihood."},"Which type of risk is NOT generally insurable?":{"Pure risk":"Pure risk offers only a chance of loss or no loss (never gain), which is exactly the type insurers cover.","Static risk":"Static risks (unchanging, from natural or human causes) are predictable and insurable.","Fundamental risk":"Fundamental risks affect large groups but can still be insured (often via pools or government programs like flood/quake)."},"For property and casualty insurance, insurable interest must exist:":{"Only at the time the policy is issued":"That is the LIFE insurance rule. P&C requires insurable interest at the time of loss too, since it indemnifies actual loss.","At the time of loss only":"P&C requires interest at BOTH issuance and loss, not loss alone.","Neither; it is optional":"Insurable interest is never optional; without it the contract is a wager and unenforceable."},"Under the risk management matrix, a low-frequency, high-severity risk (like a catastrophic liability lawsuit) should be handled by:":{"Risk retention":"Retention suits low-severity risks you can absorb. A catastrophic loss is too severe to retain.","Risk avoidance":"Avoidance is for HIGH-frequency, high-severity risks. A rare (low-frequency) event doesn't warrant avoiding the activity entirely.","Risk reduction":"Reduction (loss control) fits high-frequency, low-severity risks; it lessens odds/size but won't handle a catastrophic exposure."},"A broker represents which party in an insurance transaction?":{"The insurance company (insurer)":"That describes an AGENT. A broker represents the buyer, not the insurer, and cannot bind coverage.","The state insurance commissioner":"The commissioner is the state regulator, not a party a broker represents in placing coverage.","The NAIC":"The NAIC is a coordinating body of state regulators; brokers work for the prospective insured."},"An insurance contract is considered \"aleatory\" because:":{"It can be cancelled at any time":"Cancellation rights don't define aleatory; this describes a conditional/cancelable feature, not the chance element.","The insurer prepares it on a take-it-or-leave-it basis":"That is ADHESION, not aleatory.","Only one party can enforce it in court":"That is the UNILATERAL characteristic, not aleatory."},"Which is the most important factor in underwriting disability income insurance?":{"The applicant's age":"Age matters but occupation is primary, since job duties drive the true injury/illness exposure.","The applicant's credit score":"Credit isn't the key DI underwriting factor; occupational risk class is.","The policy face amount":"Face/benefit amount is limited by income replacement, not the top underwriting factor; occupation is."},"Which of the four elements of negligence requires an unbroken chain of events linking the breach to the harm?":{"Duty":"Duty is the obligation owed to another; it doesn't address the causal chain linking breach to harm.","Breach":"Breach is the failure to meet the duty, not the causal connection to the resulting damages.","Damages":"Damages are the actual harm suffered; proximate cause is what links the breach to those damages."},"Which organization rates the financial strength of insurance companies?":{"The NAIC":"The NAIC writes model laws and coordinates state regulation; it does NOT rate insurer financial strength.","The state insurance commissioner":"Commissioners regulate/license insurers; independent agencies like A.M. Best assign strength ratings.","FINRA":"FINRA oversees broker-dealers and securities, not insurer financial-strength ratings."},"An insurer continues to accept premiums after learning of a policy violation, then later tries to deny a claim based on that same violation. This is prevented by:":{"Subrogation":"Subrogation lets an insurer recover from a third party after paying a claim; it doesn't stop the insurer from denying based on a known violation.","Reformation":"Reformation corrects a contract to reflect true intent; it doesn't address giving up a right by accepting premiums.","Parol evidence rule":"Parol evidence bars outside evidence contradicting a written contract; irrelevant to waiving a denial right."},"Which HO form provides open-perils coverage on BOTH the dwelling AND personal property?":{"HO-2":"HO-2 is named-perils (broad form) on both the dwelling and contents, not open-perils.","HO-3":"HO-3 gives open perils on the dwelling but only named perils on personal property.","HO-4":"HO-4 is the renters/tenants form covering contents on named perils, with no dwelling coverage."},"Which peril is EXCLUDED from standard HO policies but can be obtained through NFIP?":{"Fire":"Fire is a core covered peril on all HO forms, not an exclusion.","Wind damage":"Wind is generally covered (may be sub-limited in coastal areas), not an NFIP peril.","Vandalism":"Vandalism/malicious mischief is a covered named peril on standard HO forms."},"Personal property coverage under a standard HO policy is set at what percentage of Coverage A?":{"10%":"10% is Coverage B (other structures), not Coverage C personal property.","25%":"25% approximates the Coverage D (loss of use) default on some forms, not personal property.","80%":"80% is the coinsurance/replacement-cost threshold for the dwelling, not the Coverage C percentage."},"Under a PLUP, the homeowners or auto policy that responds first acts as a:":{"Supplemental payer":"The underlying policy pays FIRST (primary), and the umbrella supplements it, not the reverse.","Co-insurer":"The layers are stacked (primary then umbrella), not sharing a loss proportionally as co-insurers.","Reinsurer":"Reinsurance is insurance an insurer buys to spread its own risk, not the underlying policy's role to the insured."},"Part C of the PAP covers:":{"Collision damage to your vehicle":"Physical damage to your own auto is Part D, not Part C.","Medical payments for all occupants":"Medical payments coverage is Part B.","Liability to third parties":"Bodily injury/property damage liability is Part A."},"A professional liability policy for a CFP certificant is also known as:":{"General liability":"General liability covers bodily injury/property damage to third parties (slips, premises), not negligent professional advice.","Umbrella liability":"An umbrella adds excess personal liability limits; it doesn't cover professional errors in giving advice.","Product liability":"Product liability covers harm from a defective product, not a service professional's advice."},"Which HO form is designed for condominium unit owners?":{"HO-2":"HO-2 is the broad named-perils form for a house owner, not a condo unit.","HO-4":"HO-4 is the renters/tenants form; condo owners use HO-6.","HO-8":"HO-8 is the modified form for older homes where replacement cost exceeds market value, not condos."},"An HO-15 endorsement upgrades which coverage from named perils to open perils?":{"Coverage A (dwelling)":"On an HO-3 the dwelling is already open perils; the HO-15 upgrades the contents, not the dwelling.","Coverage B (other structures)":"Other structures follow the dwelling's open-perils basis; the endorsement targets personal property.","Coverage E (liability)":"Coverage E is personal liability, not a property peril basis, so it isn't upgraded to open perils."},"A matching pair of antique vases is insured; one is destroyed in a fire. Under the pair or set clause, the insurer pays:":{"The full replacement cost of the entire pair":"The clause pays only the reduction in value, not the cost to replace the whole undamaged pair.","Nothing, since one piece survived":"A partial loss to a set is compensable; the insurer pays the lost value, not nothing.","Half the appraised value of the pair":"Payment is the actual difference in value the loss causes, which isn't necessarily half."},"A client desires flexibility in both premiums and death benefit and has a moderate risk tolerance. Which policy is MOST appropriate?":{"Whole life":"Whole life has fixed premiums and a fixed death benefit; it offers no flexibility.","Term life":"Term has level premiums for a set period and no cash value or adjustable death benefit.","Variable life (VL)":"VL adds investment control and market risk, better for HIGH risk tolerance; premiums/DB flexibility is UL's hallmark."},"Under UL Option B (increasing death benefit), the Net Amount at Risk (NAR) is:":{"Decreasing as cash value grows":"That's Option A (level death benefit), where DB = face and NAR shrinks as cash grows.","Always equal to the face amount":"Under Option B the death benefit equals face PLUS cash value, so it exceeds face; NAR stays level.","Equal to zero at retirement":"The NAR (the pure insurance layer) never drops to zero under Option B; it stays constant as cash value grows."},"Which life insurance policy requires a prospectus and is regulated as a security?":{"Whole life":"Whole life is a fixed insurance product with guaranteed cash value; not a security.","Universal life":"Standard UL is a fixed product with an interest-crediting general account; no prospectus required.","Equity-Indexed Universal Life":"EIUL credits interest tied to an index but has a floor and is a fixed insurance product, NOT a registered security."},"A second-to-die survivorship life policy is MOST commonly used for:":{"Funding a buy-sell agreement":"Buy-sells typically use first-to-die coverage on each owner; survivorship pays only at the second death.","Covering a mortgage":"A mortgage payoff needs proceeds at the first death; second-to-die pays too late for that.","Replacing income for a surviving spouse":"Income replacement needs a benefit at the first death; second-to-die pays only after both die."},"The capital RETENTION method of life insurance needs analysis differs from capital utilization in that it:":{"Depletes the principal over the income period":"That describes capital UTILIZATION, which spends down principal by the end of the period.","Produces a smaller insurance need":"Retention preserves principal, so it produces a LARGER need, not smaller.","Uses the insured's current salary as the benefit":"The benefit is based on the income-replacement capital needed, not simply current salary."},"Which dividend option provides the GREATEST long-term increase in death benefit and cash accumulation?":{"Cash option":"Taking dividends in cash removes them from the policy, adding nothing to death benefit or cash value.","Reduce premium option":"Applying dividends to reduce premiums lowers out-of-pocket cost but doesn't grow the DB or cash value.","Accumulate at interest":"Accumulating at interest grows a side fund but adds less than paid-up additions, which buy insurance and compound dividends."},"A life insurance policy that fails the 7-pay test becomes a:":{"Term policy":"Failing the 7-pay test doesn't change the policy type; it changes the TAX treatment (MEC).","Adjustable life policy":"Adjustable life is a policy design choice, unrelated to the 7-pay premium-funding test.","Guaranteed renewable policy":"Guaranteed renewable is a renewability provision, not the result of overfunding a policy."},"Under a 1035 exchange, which transaction is NOT permitted?":{"Life insurance to life insurance":"Life-to-life is a permitted tax-free 1035 exchange.","Life insurance to annuity":"Life-to-annuity is permitted (the one-way street runs from life to annuity).","Annuity to annuity":"Annuity-to-annuity is a permitted tax-free 1035 exchange."},"A policyowner dies by suicide 14 months after policy issuance. The insurer will:":{"Pay the full death benefit":"Within the 2-year suicide clause the full benefit is NOT paid; only premiums are returned.","Pay half the death benefit":"The suicide clause returns premiums paid, not a partial death benefit.","Deny the claim and refund nothing":"The insurer must refund premiums paid; it isn't a total forfeiture."},"A planner repeatedly recommends clients replace existing life policies with new ones solely to generate fresh commissions. This practice is called:":{"Twisting":"Twisting is churning that specifically uses MISREPRESENTATION of a competitor's policy; here no misrepresentation is stated.","Rescission":"Rescission is voiding a contract back to inception (e.g., for material misrepresentation), not serial replacing for commissions.","Reformation":"Reformation corrects a contract's wording to match intent; unrelated to replacing policies for commissions."},"Which type of annuity contract results in the annuity owner bearing ALL investment risk?":{"Fixed annuity":"In a fixed annuity the insurer guarantees principal and a minimum rate, so the insurer bears the investment risk.","Immediate annuity":"Immediate refers to when payout begins, not who bears risk; an immediate fixed annuity shifts risk to the insurer.","Equity-indexed annuity":"An EIA has a floor (usually 0%), so the owner does NOT bear all downside market risk."},"An annuity that provides the highest payout per period but provides NO benefit to heirs if the annuitant dies early is a:":{"Life with 10-year period certain":"The period-certain feature guarantees payments to heirs for 10 years, lowering the payout below life-only.","Joint and survivor annuity":"J&S continues to a second life, so it pays the LOWEST amount and does leave a survivor benefit.","Period certain only":"Period certain pays for a fixed term to heirs if death is early; it isn't the highest lifetime payout."},"Non-qualified annuity withdrawals before annuitization are taxed under:":{"FIFO (basis first, then gains)":"FIFO applies to pre-8/14/1982 contracts; modern non-qualified annuity withdrawals are LIFO (gains first).","Pro-rata exclusion ratio":"The exclusion ratio applies to ANNUITIZED payments, not pre-annuitization withdrawals.","Capital gains rates":"Annuity gains are taxed as ordinary income, never at capital gains rates."},"An equity-indexed annuity (EIA) differs from a variable annuity in that the EIA:":{"Is registered as a security with the SEC":"An EIA is a fixed insurance product, NOT registered as a security; the variable annuity is.","Bears all market risk by the owner":"The EIA has a floor and caps, so the owner does not bear all market risk; the variable annuity's owner does.","Requires a prospectus":"A prospectus is required for the VARIABLE annuity (a security), not the fixed EIA."},"Which statement about non-qualified annuities is CORRECT?":{"All distributions are fully taxable":"Only the gain is taxable; the after-tax basis comes back tax-free.","They are funded with pre-tax dollars":"Non-qualified annuities are funded with AFTER-tax dollars; that's why basis returns tax-free.","They are subject to required minimum distributions (RMDs) at age 73":"RMD rules apply to qualified plans/IRAs; non-qualified annuities have no RMDs in accumulation."},"A client wants to defer income (and RMDs) from a portion of their IRA until as late as age 85. The appropriate product is a:":{"SPIA":"A SPIA begins income immediately; it doesn't defer income or RMDs to age 85.","Variable annuity":"A variable annuity inside an IRA doesn't exempt that money from RMDs; only a QLAC defers RMDs to 85.","RILA":"A RILA (buffered annuity) manages market risk but offers no special RMD deferral to age 85."},"A rider that guarantees a client can withdraw a stated percentage annually for life, even if the account value reaches zero, is a:":{"GMAB":"A GMAB guarantees the ACCUMULATION value at a future date, not lifetime withdrawals after the account hits zero.","GMIB":"A GMIB guarantees a minimum income base if the client ANNUITIZES, not flexible lifetime withdrawals.","GMDB":"A GMDB guarantees a minimum DEATH benefit to heirs, not living withdrawals."},"An employee is terminated involuntarily. Under COBRA, health insurance continuation coverage is available for:":{"12 months":"12 months isn't a COBRA period; involuntary termination gives 18 months.","29 months":"29 months applies only when a disability extension is granted, not for a standard involuntary termination.","36 months":"36 months applies to dependent qualifying events (death, divorce, loss of dependent status), not termination."},"Medicare Part A is typically available without a premium if the enrollee has:":{"20 quarters of covered employment":"20 quarters isn't enough; premium-free Part A requires 40 quarters (10 years).","30 quarters of covered employment":"30 quarters yields only a REDUCED Part A premium, not premium-free coverage.","Paid into Medicare for at least 20 years":"The measure is 40 quarters (10 years), not 20 years of contributions."},"Which Medicare plan is an HMO-style plan requiring a gatekeeper PCP and in-network care (except emergencies)?":{"Medicare Part A":"Part A is hospital/inpatient coverage under Original Medicare, not a managed-care HMO plan.","Medicare Part B":"Part B is outpatient/physician coverage under Original Medicare, with no gatekeeper network.","Medicare Medigap Plan G":"Medigap supplements Original Medicare and imposes no network/gatekeeper; it isn't an HMO plan."},"The IRMAA surcharge on Medicare Part B premiums is based on:":{"Current year income":"IRMAA uses a lookback, not current-year income, since current income isn't yet filed.","Prior year income":"It's TWO years prior, not the immediately prior year (e.g., 2025 uses 2023 MAGI).","Lifetime earnings":"Lifetime earnings drive the Social Security benefit, not the Medicare IRMAA surcharge."},"Children of a married employee may remain on a parent's health plan under the ACA until:":{"Age 18 if unmarried":"The ACA extends coverage to age 26, and marital status is irrelevant.","Age 21 regardless of status":"The ACA threshold is age 26, not 21.","Age 26 only if unmarried":"Marital status doesn't matter; a married child can stay to age 26 (though the child's spouse can't be added)."},"A Medicare Supplement (Medigap) policy CANNOT be used with:":{"Original Medicare Part A":"Medigap is designed to supplement Part A; it works fine with it.","Original Medicare Part B":"Medigap supplements Part B as well; that's its purpose.","Both Parts A and B":"Medigap is meant to be paired with both Parts A and B; that combination is exactly where it works."},"An employee is terminated for gross misconduct. Under COBRA, the employee and dependents are:":{"Eligible for 18 months of coverage":"Gross misconduct is NOT a qualifying event, so no 18-month continuation applies.","Eligible for 36 months of coverage":"36 months is for dependent events; gross-misconduct termination triggers no COBRA at all.","Eligible only if the employer has 50+ employees":"COBRA's threshold is 20+ employees, but more importantly gross misconduct disqualifies eligibility entirely."},"A retiree appeals an IRMAA surcharge after a recent divorce reduced their household income. This is:":{"Not permitted under any circumstance":"IRMAA appeals ARE allowed for qualifying life-changing events, including divorce.","Only permitted for retirement, not divorce":"Divorce is expressly one of the listed life-changing events, alongside retirement.","Automatically applied without an appeal needed":"IRMAA relief for a life-changing event requires filing Form SSA-44; it isn't automatic."},"A trial attorney becomes too disabled to handle court appearances but could work as a law professor. Under the OWN OCCUPATION definition, she would:":{"NOT receive benefits because she can work in another occupation":"That's the ANY-occupation definition. Own-occupation pays because she can't perform HER own job.","Receive partial benefits based on income loss":"Own-occ pays FULL benefits when she can't do her own job; income-loss proration is residual disability.","Receive benefits only for the first 2 years":"A 2-year limit describes a modified/split definition, not a true own-occupation policy."},"A disability income policy that the insurer cannot cancel AND cannot increase premiums from the stated contract amounts is called:":{"Guaranteed renewable":"Guaranteed renewable can't be cancelled, but the insurer CAN raise premiums by class; noncancelable locks the premium.","Conditionally renewable":"Conditionally renewable lets the insurer non-renew on stated conditions, offering weaker protection.","Optionally renewable":"Optionally renewable gives the insurer the right to non-renew at anniversaries, the least favorable to the insured."},"If a business owner's disability income policy premiums are paid with after-tax personal dollars, the disability benefits received are:":{"Fully taxable as ordinary income":"Benefits are taxable only when premiums were paid PRE-tax (e.g., by the employer); after-tax premiums yield tax-free benefits.","Taxable only above the MOOP":"MOOP is a health-insurance out-of-pocket cap, unrelated to DI benefit taxation.","Subject to capital gains tax":"DI benefits are never capital gains; if taxable they'd be ordinary income, but here they're tax-free."},"Business Overhead Expense (BOE) insurance would cover which of the following?":{"The disabled owner's personal salary":"BOE excludes the owner's own salary/draw; personal income is covered by individual DI.","The buyout of a partner's share":"Buying out a disabled partner is the job of disability buy-sell insurance, not BOE.","Personal disability income replacement":"Personal income replacement is individual DI; BOE reimburses fixed business overhead only."},"Long-term care insurance benefits are triggered when an insured cannot perform how many Activities of Daily Living (ADLs)?":{"1 of 6":"One ADL isn't enough; the tax-qualified trigger is inability to perform 2 of 6 ADLs.","3 of 6":"Three is stricter than the standard; the HIPAA-qualified trigger is 2 of 6 ADLs.","4 of 6":"Four ADLs exceeds the required trigger; only 2 of 6 (or cognitive impairment) is needed."},"A DI policy that pays benefits proportional to the actual loss of income (e.g., 40% income loss = 40% benefit) for the entire benefit period is providing:":{"Partial disability benefits":"Partial disability typically pays a flat 50% for a short 3-6 month window, not a loss-proportional benefit for the full period.","Total disability benefits":"Total disability pays the full benefit when unable to work; it isn't prorated to a percentage of income loss.","Presumptive disability benefits":"Presumptive disability pays full benefits automatically for specified losses (sight, hearing, limbs), not a proportional amount."},"Under the Social Insurance Supplement (SIS) rider, how does a Social Security disability benefit affect the DI policy payment?":{"It has no effect on the DI benefit":"Wrong — the whole point of a SIS rider is that it coordinates with (offsets) SS disability, so it does affect the payment.","It doubles the DI benefit":"Backwards — SIS reduces, not increases, the DI benefit; it never stacks or doubles payments.","The SS benefit is taxed because the DI covers it":"Irrelevant confusion — SIS coordinates benefit amounts; it does not create taxation of the Social Security benefit."},"A factory worker loses sight in both eyes in an accident. Under a presumptive disability provision, benefits:":{"Begin only after the standard elimination period":"That's the normal claim rule; presumptive disability specifically waives the elimination period for qualifying losses.","Are denied because the worker can still perform some duties":"Presumptive losses (sight/hearing/speech/limbs) are deemed total disability automatically — no occupational-duty test applies.","Are limited to 50% of the full benefit":"Presumptive disability pays the full total-disability benefit, not a reduced or partial amount."},"A 2% S-corp shareholder-employee has DI premiums paid by the corporation and passed through as W-2 income. If they become disabled, the benefits received are:":{"Fully taxable":"That's the rule when the employer pays with pre-tax dollars; here the premium was taxed as W-2 income, so benefits are tax-free.","Taxed at capital gains rates":"DI benefits are never capital gains — they are ordinary income when taxable, tax-free when premiums were after-tax.","Taxable only above the standard deduction":"No such threshold rule for DI benefits; taxability turns on who paid the premium and with what dollars."},"A veteran enlisted in 2005 needs to meet which service requirement for VA benefits eligibility?":{"12 continuous months of active duty":"Wrong threshold — post-1980 enlistees generally need 24 continuous months, not 12.","18 continuous months of active duty":"Wrong threshold — the requirement is 24 continuous months for those enlisting after Sept 7, 1980.","No minimum service requirement applies":"A minimum does apply for post-1980 enlistees; only certain qualifying discharges waive the 24-month rule."},"Which employee benefit account requires enrollment in a High-Deductible Health Plan (HDHP)?":{"FSA (Flexible Spending Arrangement)":"An FSA has no HDHP requirement; it's employer-sponsored but works with any health plan (and can't be paired with an HSA).","HRA (Health Reimbursement Arrangement)":"HRAs are employer-funded and do not require an HDHP; only the HSA has that condition.","Section 125 Cafeteria Plan":"A cafeteria plan is just the pre-tax funding vehicle for benefits — it carries no HDHP enrollment requirement."},"A group disability plan uses the \"own occupation\" definition for the first 2 years, then switches to \"any occupation.\" This is called a:":{"Residual definition":"Residual/partial disability pays a proportional benefit for lost income — it's not about changing the occupation definition over time.","Modified own occupation":"Modified own-occ blends own- and any-occ criteria at once; the split definition instead changes definitions at a set point in time.","Presumptive disability provision":"Presumptive disability auto-qualifies specific losses — unrelated to the own-occ-then-any-occ transition described."},"Which of the following is TRUE regarding a Flexible Spending Arrangement (FSA)?":{"Funds roll over indefinitely":"False — FSAs are use-it-or-lose-it; at most a grace period or a limited carryover applies, never indefinite rollover.","Employees can contribute unlimited amounts":"False — health FSA salary-reduction contributions are capped by an annual IRS limit.","FSAs are portable if employment ends":"False — FSAs are employer-owned and not portable; balances are generally forfeited at termination."},"Workers' compensation is characterized as the:":{"Optional coverage that employers can elect":"In nearly every state workers' comp is mandatory, not elective, for covered employers.","Employee-funded program through payroll deductions":"It is employer-funded; employees do not pay premiums via payroll deduction.","Federal program administered by the Department of Labor":"Workers' comp is state-mandated and state-administered, not a federal DOL program."},"Group term life insurance premiums paid by an employer for coverage NOT exceeding $50,000 are:":{"Deductible by the employer and taxable to the employee":"The employer deducts, but under Section 79 the first $50,000 of coverage is tax-free to the employee, not taxable.","Non-deductible by the employer but tax-free to the employee":"Backwards on the employer side — the premium IS deductible to the employer.","Taxable to both employer and employee":"Neither is taxed here — the employer deducts the cost and the employee excludes coverage up to $50,000."},"A Section 125 cafeteria plan allows employees to pay for qualified benefits with:":{"After-tax dollars only":"Opposite of the benefit — Section 125's entire purpose is to allow PRE-tax payment for qualified benefits.","Employer-matched dollars":"A cafeteria plan lets employees redirect their own salary pre-tax; it isn't an employer-match arrangement.","Post-retirement dollars":"Cafeteria plans fund current-year benefits from current salary, not post-retirement dollars."},"A large employer wants to bear its own claims risk while limiting state insurance law exposure under ERISA preemption. The appropriate funding method is:":{"Fully-insured":"A fully-insured plan transfers claims risk to an insurer and remains subject to state insurance regulation — the opposite of what's wanted.","Level-funded":"Level-funded is a hybrid with an insurer bearing much of the risk via stop-loss/premium smoothing — less ERISA-preemption benefit than pure self-funding.","VEBA-only funding":"A VEBA is a tax-exempt funding trust, not the self-insurance structure that secures ERISA preemption for a self-funded plan."},"A key employee is covered under a group term life plan that discriminates in their favor. How much must they include in taxable income?":{"Only the cost above $50,000, per the normal Section 79 rule":"The $50,000 exclusion is LOST for a favored key employee under a discriminatory plan — that normal rule no longer applies.","Nothing, since key employees are always exempt":"The opposite — discrimination penalizes the key employee, forcing full inclusion, not exemption.","Half of the employer-paid premium":"No 50% rule exists; the favored key employee includes the ENTIRE employer-paid premium."},"Which workers&rsquo; compensation benefit type represents approximately one-third of all benefits paid?":{"Death benefits":"Death benefits are a relatively small share of comp payouts, not one-third.","Total permanent disability":"Permanent total disability is a smaller portion; medical expenses make up roughly one-third.","Partial temporary disability":"Temporary partial wage benefits are not the ~one-third share — that's medical expense benefits."},"Ricky, age 32, has a clean driving record. He recently purchased a car for $1,500. What is the most appropriate risk management technique for handling the collision exposure of Ricky’s car?":{"Avoidance":"Avoidance means not owning/driving the car at all — impractical here; a low-value exposure is best simply retained.","Transfer":"Buying collision coverage (transfer) on a $1,500 car is inefficient — premiums/deductible outweigh the small potential loss.","Diversification":"Diversification spreads investment risk; it isn't a technique for a single-asset collision exposure."},"What is the type of authority that an insurance company does not expressly give to agents, but agents normally possess because it is reasonably necessary for them to carry out their duties?":{"Apparent authority":"Apparent authority arises from the insurer's conduct leading a third party to believe authority exists — not from what's reasonably necessary to the job.","Express authority":"Express authority is specifically granted, usually in writing — the opposite of authority the company did NOT expressly give.","Ultimate authority":"Not a recognized agency-authority category in insurance law."},"Which of the following statements regarding the roles of the NAIC in regulating insurance is CORRECT? I. Through the NAIC, the 50 state Commissioners exchange information and ideas and coordinate regulatory activities. II. The NAIC makes recommendations for legislation and policy. III. The NAIC has the legal authority to require that states adopt its regulations.":{"I only":"Too narrow — the NAIC also makes legislative/policy recommendations (II), so I alone is incomplete.","III only":"III is false — the NAIC has no legal authority to force states to adopt its model regulations.","I, II, and III":"Includes false statement III; the NAIC coordinates and recommends but cannot compel state adoption."},"Jacob negligently strikes Gary’s car and causes considerable property damage. In turn, Gary’s insurance company reimburses him for this loss. Which legal doctrine permits Gary’s insurance company to sue Jacob to recoup its losses?":{"The collateral source rule":"That rule bars reducing a plaintiff's recovery by other-source payments — it doesn't let the insurer pursue the at-fault party.","The rescission rule":"Rescission voids a policy for fraud/misrepresentation by the insured — unrelated to recovering from a negligent third party.","The right of reformation 28":"Reformation amends a contract to reflect the parties' true intent — it's not a recovery action against Jacob."},"Which statement correctly describes a contract of indemnity?":{"Contracts of indemnity will reimburse the insured only for a small portion of the actual amount of the loss.":"Indemnity restores the insured up to the full actual loss, not merely a small portion.","Contracts of indemnity will reimburse the insured for more than":"Indemnity prevents profiting from a loss — it never pays MORE than the actual loss.","Contracts of indemnity will not reimburse the insured for any amount.":"Indemnity does pay — up to the actual loss; it isn't a no-payment contract."},"Which statement regarding the legal characteristics of insurance contracts is CORRECT? I. Insurance contracts are unilateral contracts. II. Insurance contracts are contracts of adhesion meaning that the policyowner can only accept or reject the contract and cannot modify its terms. III. Insurance contracts are aleatory contracts meaning that the outcome is affected by chance and the dollars collected by the parties must be equal.":{"I only":"Too narrow — statement II (adhesion) is also correct, so I alone is incomplete.","I, II, and III":"III is false — aleatory means dollars exchanged are UNEQUAL, not required to be equal.","II and III":"III is false (dollars are unequal), and this omits correct statement I."},"XYZ Food Company had a reckless employee who was driving a company-owned truck too fast and, as a result, hit a picket fence, knocked down several mail boxes, and crashed into an oak tree. XYZ Food Company’s insurer paid several thousand dollars in damages for these losses. Which term best describes XYZ’s position?":{"Comparative negligence":"That's a defense allocating fault between plaintiff and defendant — not the employer's responsibility for its employee's acts.","Contributory negligence":"That bars an injured party's recovery for their own fault — unrelated to the company being liable for its driver.","Assumption of the risk":"A defense where a plaintiff knowingly accepted a danger — doesn't describe the employer's liability for the employee."},"Which of the following terms is correctly described?":{"Morale hazard: lack of confidence that a potential loss is adequately covered.":"Misdefined — a morale hazard is carelessness/indifference toward loss because one is insured, not a feeling about coverage.","Adverse selection: a standard underwriting approach so those that most need insurance can receive it.":"Backwards — adverse selection is the tendency of high-risk applicants to seek coverage, something insurers guard AGAINST.","Underwriting process: the process of ensuring claims are settled equitably.":"That describes claims adjusting; underwriting is the process of evaluating and selecting/pricing risks."},"Which of the following is not considered a rating agency?":{"Standard & Poor’s":"S&P is a recognized insurer financial-strength rating agency.","Moody’s":"Moody's is a recognized insurer rating agency.","A.M. Best 39":"A.M. Best is the classic insurance-company rating agency."},"Which peril is included as basic coverage in most homeowners policies?":{"Water damage":"Flood/water damage is generally excluded from a standard HO policy and needs separate coverage.","Earth movement":"Earthquake/earth movement is a standard exclusion requiring a separate policy or endorsement.","Ordinance or law":"Ordinance-or-law costs are excluded under the base policy; coverage requires an endorsement."},"Personal property is covered under all forms of homeowners policies. Which item is considered to be personal property as defined by the standard homeowners policy?":{"A motorcycle":"Motorized vehicles are excluded from HO personal property coverage (covered by auto policies instead).","A pet":"Animals/pets are specifically excluded from personal property coverage.","A credit card":"Cards are excluded as personal property, though limited loss-from-unauthorized-use coverage may apply separately."},"Which common peril is excluded from coverage in most homeowners policies?":{"Power surge":"A sudden power surge is generally a covered peril; it's the power FAILURE that's excluded.","Tornado":"Tornado (windstorm) is a covered peril under standard homeowners policies.","Volcanic eruption":"Volcanic eruption is actually a named covered peril; power failure is the excluded one."},"Under a homeowners policy, which types of property are excluded or have limited coverage and should, therefore, be singled out for coverage under a separate policy? I. Recreational vehicles II. Boats III. Trailers IV. Furs":{"I and II":"Incomplete — trailers (III) and furs (IV) are also excluded/limited and need scheduling.","I and IV":"Incomplete — boats (II) and trailers (III) are likewise limited and should be scheduled.","II, III, and IV":"Omits recreational vehicles (I), which are also excluded/limited under the HO policy."},"Edward recently built a house with a replacement cost of $200,000 and an actual cash value (ACV) of $150,000. He purchased insurance on the house providing Coverage A protection of $150,000. The roof of Edward’s house has been damaged by fire. The determination has been made that the roof was 25% depreciated and that the cost for full replacement will be $20,000. What amount will the insurance company pay for the loss?":{"$18,750":"Right coinsurance math ($20,000 × 0.9375) but forgets the policy deductible is still subtracted.","$20,000":"That's full replacement cost — ignores the 80% coinsurance penalty (carried only 93.75%) and the deductible.","$20,000 less the deductible":"Skips the coinsurance penalty; Edward under-insured, so the loss is multiplied by 0.9375 before the deductible."},"Which item is always covered by a personal liability umbrella policy (PLUP)?":{"Business liability":"A PLUP never covers business liability — a separate commercial policy is required.","Watercraft liability":"Watercraft coverage is optional under a PLUP; it may or may not be included, so not 'always.'","Aircraft liability":"Aircraft liability is not guaranteed under a PLUP and is commonly excluded or optional."},"Comprehensive coverage pays for damages to the insured’s automobile from all of these perils EXCEPT":{"hail.":"Hail is a classic comprehensive (other-than-collision) peril — it IS covered by comprehensive.","collision with a deer.":"Hitting an animal is treated as comprehensive, not collision — so comprehensive DOES pay for it.","damage from a falling tree. 72":"A falling tree is an other-than-collision loss covered by comprehensive."},"What is the general risk tolerance level of a client who is considering purchase of a variable universal life (VUL) insurance policy?":{"Low":"VUL's subaccount market exposure is unsuitable for low risk tolerance — it suits moderate-to-high tolerance.","Risk averse":"A risk-averse client should avoid VUL's investment risk; whole life or fixed products fit better.","Low to moderate":"VUL puts cash value at market risk, so it targets moderate-to-high, not low-to-moderate, tolerance."},"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of variable universal life (VUL) insurance?":{"The policy must be offered with a prospectus.":"This IS a VUL characteristic — subaccount investment options make it a security requiring a prospectus.","There are increasing or decreasing death benefits.":"This IS a VUL feature — it offers a flexible (adjustable) death benefit.","There are flexible premium payments.":"This IS a VUL feature — the 'universal' element provides premium flexibility."},"What is the planning situation in which a financial planner would typically use a second-to-die life insurance policy?":{"College education planning":"Survivorship policies pay only after both insureds die — too late and ill-suited for funding college.","Retirement planning":"Second-to-die provides estate liquidity at the second death, not retirement income.","Emergency fund planning 98":"An illiquid death-benefit-at-second-death policy is the opposite of an accessible emergency fund."},"Meaghan purchased a participating whole life insurance policy 15 years ago and now wishes to receive the policy’s cash surrender value (CSV). She gives you the following information to assess the potential taxation of the surrender: CSV: $80,000 Dividends received: $12,500 Outstanding loan: $40,000 Premiums paid: $60,000 What is the amount of cash value that is taxable to Meaghan and what is the character of this income?":{"$0":"There is a taxable gain — CSV ($80k) exceeds basis (premiums $60k less dividends $12.5k = $47.5k), so $32,500 is taxable.","$40,000 capital gain":"Wrong amount and character — life policy gains are ordinary income, and the taxable figure is $32,500, not the loan amount.","$72,500 ordinary income":"Overstates the gain — basis is reduced by dividends to $47,500; CSV minus that basis is $32,500, not $72,500."},"You have a client, Patrick, who wishes to cash surrender his existing ordinary life policy. He brings you the following nonforfeiture table included in his policy. Nonforfeiture Table (Dollar values are per $1,000 of face value) Policy Cash Surrender Reduced Paid-Up Extended Term Year-End Value Insurance Insurance 1 $0 $0 0 years 2 $0 $0 0 years 3 $10 $65 5 years 4 $25 $100 8 years 106":{"$0":"Year 3 shows $10 per $1,000 of CSV — there is a value; $0 ignores the table.","$5,000":"Uses the wrong figure — that would misread the table; CSV is $10 × 200 = $2,000.","$13,000":"Not derivable from the table — the correct CSV is $10 per $1,000 × 200 units = $2,000."},"Assume you have a client who owns a modified premium whole life insurance policy and has recently taken a large loan against the cash value. She is aware that the death benefit payable to her husband, who is the sole beneficiary of the policy, will be reduced by the amount of the loan and asks you whether she can preserve at least a portion of the original death benefit. The policy is a participating policy. What is the best option for the client to choose?":{"Reduced paid-up insurance option":"That's a nonforfeiture option that lowers the face amount — it reduces, not restores, the death benefit.","Cash dividend option":"Taking dividends in cash does nothing to replace the death benefit lost to the loan.","Have dividends applied to reduce the premium":"Reducing premiums doesn't rebuild the death benefit; only the fifth (one-year term) option adds coverage back."},"Enrico wants to ensure his life insurance policy does not lapse in the event he is sick or injured and cannot work. Which rider should he have on his policy?":{"Disability income rider":"That pays a monthly income during disability — it doesn't keep the policy in force by covering premiums.","Accidental death rider":"That adds a benefit for accidental death; it does nothing to prevent lapse during disability.","Return of premium rider":"That refunds premiums at term end — unrelated to keeping the policy in force while disabled."},"Which method of calculating the life insurance need is designed to use the interest only on a portion of the death benefit for income replacement?":{"The capital utilization method":"Capital utilization spends down BOTH principal and interest over time — it doesn't preserve capital using interest only.","The LIFE method":"The LIFE method is an overall needs-analysis approach, not an interest-only income calculation.","The multiple of salary method":"A quick salary-multiple rule of thumb — not a method based on living off interest only."},"Jason, age 35, is married with children and has only a limited amount of disposable income. He needs life insurance protection but wants to keep his premium payments as low as possible for the next 20 years. He anticipates being in good health throughout this period. Assuming he remains healthy, what type of term policy would be most beneficial to Jason?":{"Decreasing term insurance":"Decreasing term shrinks the death benefit — fine for a mortgage but not the lowest-cost level protection a healthy insured can requalify for.","Annual renewable term insurance":"ART premiums climb each year, becoming costly over 20 years — reentry term rewards his continued good health with lower rates.","Modified whole life term insurance":"That's a permanent-policy hybrid with higher cost — contrary to Jason's goal of the lowest possible premiums."},"Assume that you have a client who currently has a need for permanent (cash value type) life insurance but cannot afford that form of insurance. Based on the client’s need, what is a reasonable recommendation?":{"Annual renewable term life insurance":"ART meets a temporary need but can't convert to permanent coverage — it doesn't bridge to the permanent need.","Reentry term life insurance":"Reentry term lowers cost via requalification but offers no path to permanent coverage.","Variable annuity 121":"An annuity is an accumulation/income product, not life insurance — it doesn't meet a permanent death-benefit need."},"Trisha is a financial planner for XYZ Company. She is scheduled to meet with Tim and Jodi this afternoon for a follow-up meeting. In their first meeting, Trisha learned that Tim and Jodi have three young children and no life insurance coverage. Trisha also discovered that they owe a substantial amount on their current mortgage. Moreover, Tim’s family has a history of diabetes and heart disease. What is the next planning action for Trisha?":{"She should communicate her recommendations to the clients.":"Premature — the analyze/evaluate step must come before communicating any recommendation in the planning process.","She should recommend that the clients purchase a convertible term life insurance policy for the appropriate face amount because of Tim’s family history.":"Jumps to a product recommendation before completing the required analysis-and-evaluation step.","She should recommend a decreasing term life insurance policy for both Tim and Jodi to make sure the mortgage is taken care of in the unlikely event one of them should die prematurely.":"Also skips ahead to a recommendation — analysis and evaluation of their finances must happen first."},"Which factor(s) should you consider before replacing a life insurance policy?":{"The issuing company’s A.M. Best rating and other ratings":"True but incomplete — suitability and risk tolerance also matter, so 'all of these' is the better answer.","The appropriateness of the policy for the needs of the client":"A valid factor, but not the only one — ratings and risk tolerance count too.","The client’s risk tolerance level":"A valid factor, yet ratings and policy appropriateness matter as well, so all should be considered."},"When purchased inside of an IRA, what type of annuity would NOT be required to comply with the required minimum distribution (RMD) regulations?":{"Variable annuity contract":"A standard variable annuity in an IRA is still subject to RMDs — only a QLAC gets the RMD deferral.","Indexed annuity contract":"An indexed annuity in an IRA remains RMD-subject; it's the QLAC that's carved out.","Fixed annuity contract":"A fixed annuity inside an IRA must still satisfy RMDs — the QLAC is the exception."},"Which settlement option(s) in a life insurance policy are available upon the death of the insured? Remember, life insurance settlement options are the same as annuity payout options. I. Interest only II. Life income with period certain III. Cash surrender value (CSV) IV. Joint and last survivor":{"IV only":"Too narrow — interest-only (I) and life-income-with-period-certain (II) are also death settlement options.","I and II":"Omits joint and last survivor (IV), which is also a valid death settlement option.","I, II, and III":"CSV (III) is a nonforfeiture option available while living, not a death settlement option — it's retained by the insurer at death."},"Generally, which type of annuity does NOT guarantee a specific amount of annuity payment?":{"A single premium annuity":"'Single premium' describes funding, not payout — it can still be fixed and guaranteed; variability comes from being variable.","A fixed annuity":"A fixed annuity guarantees a set payment amount — the opposite of the question's answer.","A nonqualified annuity":"'Nonqualified' refers to tax treatment, not payout certainty; it can be fixed with guaranteed payments."},"Joe and Mary, a retired married couple, have no sources of income other than the interest and dividends from investments and Social Security. At this time, Joe is considering the purchase of an immediate annuity. Joe has asked you, his financial planner, which settlement option he should select. His primary objective is to ensure that Mary is provided for subsequent to his death and that she has as much income available to her as possible. What is the best recommendation for a settlement option?":{"A joint and 50% survivor annuity":"Meets the survivor goal but cuts Mary's income in half at Joe's death — the objective is maximum income for her.","A life annuity with a 20-year period certain":"If Joe dies and the 20-year term ends, Mary could outlive the payments — no lifetime guarantee for her.","A single life annuity":"Payments stop at Joe's death — leaves Mary with nothing, contradicting the objective of providing for her."},"Which exchange transaction is NOT a tax-free exchange under IRC Section 1035?":{"Exchanging a universal life insurance policy for a variable life insurance policy":"Life-to-life is a permitted 1035 exchange — it IS tax-free.","Exchanging a variable annuity for a qualified long-term care contract":"Annuity-to-qualified-LTC is a permitted 1035 exchange — it IS tax-free.","Exchanging a variable life insurance policy for a variable annuity 152":"Life-to-annuity is allowed under 1035 — it IS tax-free; only annuity-to-life is disallowed."},"Sandra has just been informed that she is the beneficiary of her grandmother’s life insurance policy. She has decided to choose a settlement option for the $500,000 death benefit. Which settlement option would prevent the risk of superannuation?":{"Lump sum distribution":"A lump sum can be spent or exhausted before death — it doesn't guarantee lifetime income against outliving assets.","Fixed-period installments":"Payments end when the fixed period does, so Sandra could outlive them — no protection from superannuation.","Fixed-amount installments":"Payments continue only until the fund is depleted, which can happen before death — no lifetime guarantee."},"Mark, age 58, has a nonqualified variable annuity worth $455,000 with a cost basis of $325,000. He decides to withdraw $11,000 to pay off the balance of a credit card. What are the tax consequences of this transaction?":{"Long-term capital gains tax on the $11,000, plus a 10% penalty":"Wrong character — annuity earnings are taxed as ordinary income, never capital gains.","Ordinary income tax on $11,000":"Correct on ordinary income but omits the 10% early-withdrawal penalty — Mark is under 59½ with no exception.","Transaction considered a tax-free distribution":"Nonqualified annuities are taxed gains-first (LIFO), so the $11,000 is fully taxable earnings, not tax-free."},"According to FINRA, the suitability standard refers to":{"whether or not the client signed all the appropriate disclosure forms.":"A signed form is evidence but not the standard — suitability turns on whether the recommendation fits the client.","recommending only the lowest-cost investment option available.":"Cost is one factor under Rule 2111, but suitability doesn't require the absolute lowest-cost product.","matching a client’s investments with the advisor’s personal preferences and risk tolerance.":"Backwards — recommendations must align with the CLIENT's situation and goals, not the advisor's."},"The preeminent feature offered by annuities is":{"market risk protection.":"Not universal — variable annuities can lose value; the defining feature is longevity (lifetime income) protection.","creditor protection.":"Creditor protection varies by state and isn't the primary purpose — longevity risk protection is.","death benefit protection.":"Not every annuity guarantees a death benefit; the preeminent feature is protection against outliving one's money."},"Equity indexed annuities can be expected to":{"mirror the broader stock market because they invest directly in a major index.":"EIAs do NOT invest directly in the index; principal sits in a protected subaccount, so returns only track an index formula, not the market itself.","outperform the broader stock market, because the downside protection offered by annuities effectively circumvents bear markets over time.":"Downside protection is paid for with rate caps, participation limits, and fee drag, which make beating the market impossible, not guaranteed.","always provide higher returns than fixed annuities.":"In flat/down years an EIA can post a zero return, so it may underperform a fixed annuity's guaranteed rate; 'always' overstates it."},"The exercise of good business ethics is important, particularly when offering annuities because":{"ethical guidelines offer a structured way to avoid long term legal repercussions without actually admitting guilt.":"Ethics are meant to prevent misconduct, not to dodge accountability; using them as legal cover subverts their purpose.","ethical guidelines can help questionable sales practices appear consistent and principled.":"Ethics must be practiced, not used to disguise questionable practices; dressing up misconduct is the opposite of ethical conduct.","promoting ethics can improve a company’s image enough to gain the trust of conscientious customers who need to purchase annuity contracts.":"Ethics as a mere trust-building sales strategy, without real integrity, undermines their purpose; conduct must be genuine, not promotional."},"Which of the following services are NOT covered under Medicare Part A?":{"Hospice care":"Hospice care IS a covered Part A benefit, so it cannot be the exception.","Home health care services":"Home health care IS covered under Part A, so it is not the answer.","Skilled nursing facility services":"Skilled nursing facility care IS a Part A benefit (after a qualifying hospital stay), so it is not excluded."},"Which statement regarding Medicare coverage of long-term care (LTC) is CORRECT?":{"Medicare will pay for the first 90 days of coverage without limits.":"Skilled-nursing coverage is limited (100 days, with coinsurance after day 20), not 90 days without limits; this confuses hospital benefit periods.","Medicare covers persons with a life expectancy of six months or less.":"That describes the hospice benefit, not LTC; a six-month prognosis is the hospice-eligibility trigger.","Medicare covers custodial care subject to a deductible and coinsurance by the patient.":"Medicare does NOT cover custodial (nonskilled) care at all, so this is false."},"Which statement regarding insurance coverage provided by Medicare Part B is CORRECT?":{"The election to participate must be made at the time the insured is eligible for Medicare Part A and at no time thereafter.":"You can enroll later during a general enrollment period (Jan 1–Mar 31), so election is not limited to initial eligibility.","Once participants elect Part B, they must maintain the coverage until death.":"Part B is voluntary and can be dropped; it is not a lifetime lock-in.","Coverage under Part B does not require payment of a deductible by the insured.":"Part B DOES carry an annual deductible plus coinsurance, so this is false."},"Which of the following organizations would be most likely to be eligible for business overhead expense (BOE) insurance?":{"A law firm with 25 partners":"BOE targets small firms dependent on one owner; 25 partners can absorb one member's disability, so overhead continues without it.","A major multinational corporation":"A large corporation's overhead does not hinge on any single individual, so BOE (which replaces overhead if the owner is disabled) is unnecessary.","A public library 203":"A public library is a public entity, not an owner-dependent small business, so it is not a BOE candidate."},"Which of the following types of health insurance covers the cost of providing nonmedical custodial care to individuals with cognitive problems?":{"Disability income insurance":"DI replaces lost income from inability to work; it does not pay for custodial care services.","Hospital expense coverage":"This covers hospital/inpatient charges, not ongoing nonmedical custodial care.","Medical expense coverage 213":"Medical expense plans cover treatment of illness/injury, not long-term custodial (ADL/cognitive) care."},"An insured must receive care for a stated number of days before a long-term care (LTC) policy can pay benefits. This time deductible is known as":{"the delay period.":"Not the industry term; the waiting period before LTC benefits begin is the elimination period.","the benefit period.":"The benefit period is how long benefits are PAID, not the wait before they start.","the enrollment period.":"Enrollment period refers to when you can sign up for coverage, not a claims waiting period."},"A client, Lisa, had a partnership long-term care (LTC) policy with a benefit of $125,000. She was forced to use the policy’s entire benefit after she was admitted to the local nursing home. Lisa is wondering if she will qualify for Medicaid. Her partnership LTC policy’s benefit has been depleted, and she only has $97,000 in assets. Which of the following statements is CORRECT?":{"Lisa cannot qualify for Medicaid until her total assets are approximately $2,000.":"This ignores the partnership policy's asset-disregard: she may protect $125,000 (equal to benefits paid) plus ~$2,000, not spend down to $2,000.","Lisa cannot qualify for Medicaid because her total assets are not less than $90,000.":"There is no $90,000 threshold; the partnership program disregards $125,000 of assets, so her $97,000 is fully protected.","Lisa can qualify for Medicaid because she is currently living in a nursing home.":"Residing in a nursing home does not by itself confer Medicaid eligibility; the asset test (met here via the partnership disregard) is what qualifies her."},"Which statement describing the features of an HSA is CORRECT?":{"Contributions are not tax deductible.":"HSA contributions ARE tax deductible (above-the-line), so this is false.","A person can establish an HSA regardless of age.":"Once enrolled in Medicare (age 65+), a person can no longer establish or contribute to an HSA.","A person can establish an HSA regardless of the type of existing health coverage in force.":"Eligibility requires enrollment in a qualifying HDHP; other coverage disqualifies you."},"All of the following statements about an HSA are correct EXCEPT":{"inside the HSA, earnings are tax deferred.":"This is TRUE (earnings grow tax-deferred/tax-free), so it is not the exception.","HSA distributions are tax free if used to pay for qualified medical expenses.":"This is a TRUE feature of HSAs, so it is not the exception.","individuals are eligible for an HSA if they cannot be claimed as a dependent on another person’s tax return. 258":"This is a TRUE eligibility requirement, so it is not the exception."},"Self-employed persons are allowed to deduct what percentage of the amount they pay for health insurance?":{"25%":"Understates the deduction; the self-employed health insurance deduction is 100% of premiums paid.","50%":"50% is the SE tax deduction figure, not the health insurance deduction, which is 100%.","75%":"No 75% cap applies; self-employed persons deduct 100% of health (and qualified LTC) premiums."},"All of the following statements regarding a group disability policy are correct EXCEPT":{"benefits are often less in group policies, which accounts for the lower cost.":"This is a TRUE statement, so it is not the exception.","a group disability policy is less expensive than an individual policy because the risk is spread over more participants.":"True—pooling risk lowers cost, so this is not the exception.","group plans often state the benefit amount as a percentage of the employee’s compensation, while individual plans pay a specific dollar amount. 268":"True—group pays a % of pay while individual pays a set benefit, so this is not the exception."},"Which of the following classes of benefits are payable to injured workers or their dependents under the workers’ compensation laws? I. Medical expenses II. Survivors death benefits III. Total permanent disability IV. Partial temporary disability":{"I and II":"Incomplete; workers' comp also pays total permanent and partial temporary disability benefits.","I and III":"Omits survivors' death benefits and partial temporary disability, both of which are covered.","III and IV":"Omits medical expenses (about a third of all comp benefits) and survivors' benefits, both covered."},"A LIRP can be established with all of the following types of policies EXCEPT":{"whole life.":"Whole life builds guaranteed cash value, so it CAN fund a LIRP; it is not the exception.","universal life.":"UL has a cash-value account, so it can fund a LIRP; not the exception.","variable life.":"Variable life carries cash value, so it can fund a LIRP; not the exception."},"Which of the following can help reduce the cost of cyber liability insurance?":{"Employee security training":"Correct on its own but incomplete—MFA and a disaster recovery plan also lower cost, so 'all of the above' is better.","Multi-factor authentication":"True but not the only measure; training and disaster recovery also reduce risk and cost.","A disaster recovery plan":"True but incomplete; training and MFA also help, so the all-inclusive answer is correct."},"Which principle(s) of the workers’ compensation law apply in most states? I. The indemnity paid to the injured employee is partial, but is to be considered final. II. The costs for workers’ compensation benefits are funded through payroll taxes to which employees are expected to contribute. III. The benefits payable under workers’ compensation is usually a percentage of the employee’s average weekly pay.":{"I only":"Omits III (benefits as a percentage of average weekly pay), which is also true.","II only":"II is false—comp is funded by employer-paid insurance premiums, not employee payroll taxes.","I and II":"Includes false statement II (employee-funded payroll taxes); employers, not employees, fund workers' comp."},"Select the statement(s) that accurately describe(s) the Madisons’ current risk management plan. I. They have inadequate life insurance. II. If Nicholas becomes disabled, he will be taxed on his monthly benefit. III. The Madisons’ home would not be covered for a volcanic eruption. IV. The Madisons’ home would be covered for lightning damage.":{"III only":"False—an HO-3 covers volcanic eruption, and it also omits the true statements I and IV.","I, II, and III":"Includes false II and III: disability benefits are tax-free (premiums paid with after-tax dollars) and HO-3 covers volcanic eruption.","I, II, and IV":"Includes false statement II—because Nicholas paid premiums with after-tax dollars, his disability benefit is received income tax-free."},"Assume Nicholas became disabled on August 1, 2025. Calculate the disability income benefit that he would be entitled to receive from his policy in 2025.":{"$2,700":"That is only one month's benefit; after the 30-day elimination period he collects four months (Sep–Dec).","$13,500":"Counts five months of benefits; the 30-day elimination period consumes August, leaving four months.","$21,600":"Counts eight months (Aug–Dec plus extras); only Sep–Dec are payable after the elimination period."},"Nicholas’s son Ben had an unexpected injury resulting in a $2,500 hospital bill. Calculate the amount of the bill Nicholas’s medical plan will cover.":{"$660":"Miscalculates; the plan pays 80% of the post-deductible amount ($2,000), which is $1,600, not $660.","$2,300":"Subtracts only a co-pay and ignores the 80% coinsurance after the $500 deductible.","$2,500":"Ignores both the $500 deductible and the 20% coinsurance the insured must bear."},"The Madisons’ home was recently damaged by fire causing $48,000 in required repairs. Nicholas mentioned to you at your last meeting that their current homeowners insurance policy insured the home for $150,000. Calculate the amount that the insurance company will reimburse the Madisons for these damages.":{"$45,000":"Subtracts only the deductible; it ignores the coinsurance penalty for insuring below 80% of replacement cost.","$47,750":"Applies just a $250 deductible with no coinsurance penalty; underinsurance triggers the 0.8333 penalty formula.","$48,000":"Assumes full replacement cost, but the home was underinsured (below 80%), so the coinsurance penalty and deductible reduce it."},"Nicholas hits a deer on his way home from work resulting in $4,000 of damages to his car. Calculate the amount of damages that the Madisons’ automobile policy will cover.":{"$0":"Hitting an animal is covered under comprehensive (other-than-collision), so the loss is not excluded.","$3,500":"Uses a $500 deductible; the comprehensive deductible here is $250, giving $3,750.","$4,000":"Ignores the $250 comprehensive deductible the insured must absorb."},"During 2025, Nicholas’s mother Ruth passed away. At that time, they discovered that she had a nonqualified variable annuity consisting of an initial investment of $50,000 made in 2000. The current value of the annuity is $126,000, and Nicholas is the sole primary beneficiary. If Nicholas chooses to take the entire $126,000 in a lump-sum distribution to help refinance the mortgage, what would be the tax consequence?":{"$76,000 ordinary income plus a 10% penalty":"No 10% penalty applies because the distribution results from the owner's death, even though the $76,000 gain is ordinary income.","$76,000 long-term capital gain":"Annuity gains are taxed as ordinary income, not capital gain; annuities lose favorable capital-gain treatment.","$126,000 ordinary income":"Only the gain above the $50,000 basis is taxable; basis is returned tax-free, so $76,000, not $126,000."},"Ruth had a universal life insurance policy with a death benefit of $100,000. The policy had a cash value of $18,000. They noticed that she had marked death benefit option B and made Nicholas the sole beneficiary. Nicholas has decided he wants to take the cash option and invest his inheritance in a lakefront property that he feels will grow in value over the next several years. Identify the amount of money Nicholas will receive from the insurance company along with the tax consequences of this transaction.":{"Nicholas will receive $100,000 and have to pay ordinary income tax on the entire distribution.":"Wrong amount and tax: Option B pays face PLUS cash value ($118,000), and death benefits are generally income tax-free.","Nicholas will receive $100,000 and have zero tax consequences on the transaction.":"Understates the payout—Option B adds the $18,000 cash value to the $100,000 face for $118,000.","Nicholas will receive $118,000 and pay ordinary income taxes on the entire distribution.":"Amount is right, but life insurance death proceeds are received income tax-free (no transfer-for-value here)."},"Ruth had a nonqualified fixed annuity in which she had invested a total of $40,000. This contract was purchased in 1998 and has a current value of $93,000. Nicholas is the sole beneficiary. He decided to take the life income option on this portion of his inheritance to help out with the entertainment section of the budget. Nicholas’s life expectancy is 35 years, and he is to receive $500 per month for the rest of his life. Calculate the taxable amount of the monthly distribution.":{"$0":"The whole payment is not tax-free; only the exclusion-ratio portion ($95.25) returns basis, leaving $404.75 taxable.","$95.25":"That is the tax-FREE (excluded basis) portion; the taxable amount is the remaining $404.75.","$500.00":"Ignores the exclusion ratio; part of each payment ($95.25) is a tax-free return of the $40,000 basis."},"Brenden, a friend of the Madisons, visits them at their home. As he is walking up the steps to enter the house, the staircase collapses and results in his leg being broken. Choose the part(s) of the Madisons’ homeowners insurance policy that would cover Brenden’s broken leg. I. Coverage E: Personal Liability II. Coverage F: Medical Payments to Others":{"I only":"Coverage E (liability, when insured is at fault) applies, but so does Coverage F, which pays medical costs regardless of fault.","II only":"Coverage F applies, but because the broken steps make the insured at fault, Coverage E (liability) also responds.","Neither I nor II":"Both coverages respond—F pays medical bills without regard to fault and E covers the insured's liability for the injury."},"Could the Madisons set up a health savings account for the family?":{"Yes, the Madisons are eligible to set up a health savings account because they have children.":"Having children is irrelevant; HSA eligibility requires an HDHP, and their $2,500 family deductible is below the required minimum.","No, the Madisons cannot set up a health savings account because only an employer can set one up.":"An employer is not required—individuals can open an HSA; the real bar is the lack of a qualifying HDHP.","Yes, the Madisons are eligible because the family is covered by a low deductible health plan.":"A LOW deductible plan disqualifies them; HSAs require a HIGH deductible health plan (family deductible at least $3,300 for 2025)."},"Which type of risk is generally insurable?":{"Speculative risk":"Speculative risk has both loss AND gain potential (like gambling/investing), so it is uninsurable.","Dynamic risk":"Dynamic risks arise from economy-wide changes and are not insurable; only pure risk is.","Fundamental dynamic risk":"Combines two uninsurable categories; insurers cover pure, particular risks, not fundamental/dynamic ones."},"A homeowner faces a low-frequency but high-severity exposure (e.g., a house fire). Per the risk management matrix, the most appropriate technique is to:":{"Retain the risk":"Retention fits low-frequency/low-severity losses you can absorb, not a catastrophic house fire.","Avoid the activity":"Avoidance is for high-frequency/high-severity exposures; you cannot practically 'avoid' owning a home.","Reduce the activity":"Reduction suits high-frequency exposures; a rare but severe loss is best transferred to an insurer."},"Which is NOT one of the four elements of an insurable risk from the insurer's perspective?":{"A large number of homogeneous exposure units":"This IS a required element (law of large numbers), so it is not the exception.","The loss must be definite and measurable":"This IS a required element, so it is not the exception.","The loss must be fortuitous":"A fortuitous (accidental/unexpected) loss IS required, so it is not the exception."},"For property and casualty insurance, when must insurable interest exist?":{"Only when the policy is issued":"That is the LIFE insurance rule; for P&C, insurable interest must also exist at the time of loss.","Only at the time of loss":"P&C requires insurable interest both at issue and at loss, not solely at loss.","Insurable interest is not required for P&C":"P&C absolutely requires insurable interest to prevent wagering and enforce indemnity."},"A peril, the cause of a loss, is best illustrated by:":{"A frayed electrical cord":"A frayed cord is a physical HAZARD that increases the chance of loss, not the peril itself.","An icy sidewalk":"Icy conditions are a hazard raising the likelihood of a fall, not the direct cause (peril) of loss.","Storing gasoline indoors":"Storing gasoline is a hazard that increases loss potential, not a peril."},"The McCarran-Ferguson Act of 1945 established that insurance is primarily regulated by:":{"The federal government":"McCarran-Ferguson reserved most insurance regulation to the STATES, not the federal government.","The NAIC directly":"The NAIC only develops model laws; it has no direct regulatory authority—states regulate.","The courts only":"Regulation is legislative/administrative at the state level, not conducted solely by courts."},"An insurance contract drafted by the insurer that the insured must accept or reject without negotiation is characterized as a contract of:":{"Indemnity":"Indemnity is the principle of restoring pre-loss position, not the take-it-or-leave-it drafting characteristic.","Aleatory exchange":"Aleatory refers to unequal exchange based on chance, not the one-sided drafting of the contract.","Subrogation":"Subrogation is the insurer's right to pursue a third party after paying a claim, not a contract type."},"Which form of insurance is the principal exception to the principle of indemnity?":{"Homeowners insurance":"Homeowners follows indemnity—it restores the insured's pre-loss position, subject to policy limits.","Auto collision insurance":"Collision coverage is an indemnity contract, paying actual cash value or repair cost, not a set face amount.","Commercial property insurance":"Commercial property is indemnity-based, reimbursing actual loss rather than a stated benefit."},"Which is NOT one of the four elements required to prove negligence?":{"A duty is owed":"Duty owed IS one of the four elements, so it is not the exception.","The duty was breached":"Breach of duty IS a required element, so it is not the exception.","Actual damages occurred":"Actual damages IS a required element, so it is not the exception."},"Under a pure contributory negligence rule, an injured plaintiff who was partly at fault will:":{"Recover damages reduced by their percentage of fault":"That describes COMPARATIVE negligence; pure contributory bars recovery entirely for any plaintiff fault.","Recover full damages":"Any fault by the plaintiff bars recovery under contributory negligence—no full recovery.","Recover only medical costs":"Contributory negligence bars all recovery for a partly-at-fault plaintiff, not just non-medical damages."},"An agent's authority that arises because it is necessary to transact business (such as collecting premiums), though not expressly stated, is:":{"Express authority":"Express authority is what is specifically WRITTEN in the agency contract, not what is merely necessary.","Apparent authority":"Apparent authority is based on how things appear to a third party, not on business necessity.","Ratified authority":"Ratification is after-the-fact approval of an unauthorized act, not authority necessary to do business."},"An insurance broker legally represents:":{"The insurer":"That describes an agent; a broker represents the buyer and cannot bind the insurer.","The NAIC":"The NAIC is a regulatory association; a broker represents the insurance buyer.","Both parties equally":"A broker owes its duty to the buyer, not equally to both sides."},"A client who is careless because \"it's insured anyway\" exhibits a:":{"Moral hazard":"Moral hazard involves dishonesty/fraud (e.g., faking a claim); mere carelessness because it's insured is a MORALE hazard.","Physical peril":"Carelessness is an attitude, not a physical cause of loss; and 'peril' is a cause, not a hazard.","Speculative risk":"Speculative risk involves chance of gain or loss; this is a hazard describing insured indifference."},"Which rating agency is the oldest and specializes only in rating insurance companies?":{"Standard & Poor's":"S&P rates many types of debt and firms broadly, not solely insurers, and is not the oldest insurance-specific rater.","Moody's":"Moody's is a general credit-rating agency, not the insurance-only specialist; A.M. Best is.","Fitch Ratings":"Fitch is a broad credit-rating agency and the youngest of the majors, not the insurance-only specialist."},"Under a homeowners policy, Coverage D provides:":{"Coverage for the dwelling":"That is Coverage A (dwelling), not Coverage D.","Coverage for detached structures":"Detached/other structures are Coverage B, not Coverage D.","Medical payments to others":"Medical payments to others is Coverage F, not Coverage D (loss of use)."},"Which homeowners form is the most widely purchased and provides open perils on the dwelling but named perils on personal property?":{"HO-2":"HO-2 (Broad Form) is named-perils on BOTH dwelling and contents, not open-perils on the dwelling.","HO-4":"HO-4 is the renters/tenants form covering contents only, with no dwelling coverage.","HO-8":"HO-8 is the modified/older-home form using actual cash value, not the popular open-peril HO-3."},"A renter who wants coverage for personal property, liability, and loss of use (but not the building) should buy:":{"HO-3":"HO-3 insures the dwelling (Coverages A/B), which a renter does not own and does not need.","HO-6":"HO-6 is for condo unit-owners (includes limited building/interior coverage), not renters.","HO-8":"HO-8 is the modified form for older homes the owner occupies, not a renters/tenants policy."},"Under standard homeowners policies, Coverage C (personal property) is typically what percentage of Coverage A?":{"10%":"10% is the typical Coverage B (other structures) limit, not Coverage C.","25%":"25%–30% is a common Coverage D (loss of use) figure, not Coverage C, which is 50%.","80%":"80% is the coinsurance requirement for full replacement-cost recovery, not the Coverage C limit."},"To have partial losses paid in full, a homeowner must insure the dwelling for at least what percentage of replacement cost?":{"50%":"Too low; the coinsurance requirement for full partial-loss payment is 80% of replacement cost.","70%":"Not the standard threshold; the 80% coinsurance rule governs partial-loss recovery.","100%":"Full replacement value isn't required for partial losses—80% satisfies the coinsurance rule (100% is needed to avoid any gap on total losses)."},"The predominant provider of residential flood insurance is:":{"Private insurers through the HO-3":"Flood is EXCLUDED from HO-3 and all standard homeowners forms, so it isn't provided this way.","The California Earthquake Authority":"The CEA covers earthquakes in California, not flood insurance.","Lloyd's of London":"Lloyd's writes specialty/surplus lines but is not the predominant U.S. residential flood provider; the NFIP is."},"Coverage F (medical payments to others) under a homeowners policy:":{"Requires proof of negligence by the insured":"Coverage F pays regardless of fault—no proof of negligence is needed (that's the Coverage E liability standard).","Pays for injury to household members":"Coverage F excludes the insured's own household/resident family members; it covers non-residents.","Covers the insured's own medical bills":"Coverage F pays medical bills of OTHERS, not the insured's own injuries."},"A personal liability umbrella policy (PLUP) typically:":{"Replaces the homeowners and auto policies":"An umbrella supplements, not replaces, the underlying HO/auto policies, which must stay in force.","Pays before the homeowners and auto policies":"The umbrella pays only AFTER the underlying HO/auto limits are exhausted, not first.","Covers only the insured's medical bills":"A PLUP provides broad excess LIABILITY coverage, not the insured's own medical costs."},"Which Personal Auto Policy coverage is mandatory in essentially all states?":{"Medical payments (Part B)":"Med-pay is optional first-party coverage, not the mandatory part; liability is.","Uninsured motorist (Part C)":"UM is required in some states but not essentially all; liability (Part A) is the universal requirement.","Collision (Part D)":"Collision is optional (often lender-required), not state-mandated; liability is the mandatory coverage."},"In the auto split limit 25/50/10, the \"50\" represents:":{"Bodily injury per person":"That is the first number (25) — the per-person BI cap, not the middle number.","Property damage per accident":"That is the last number (10), not the middle figure.","The deductible":"Split limits express liability caps, not a deductible; the deductible applies to your own collision/comprehensive."},"Damage to your auto from hitting a deer would be covered under:":{"Collision":"Collision covers impact with another vehicle or object; contact with an animal is specifically classified as other-than-collision.","Liability":"Liability pays others for damage you cause, not damage to your own auto.","Uninsured motorist":"UM applies when an at-fault uninsured driver injures you, not an animal strike."},"Which life insurance type guarantees the premium, death benefit, and cash value?":{"Annually renewable term":"Term has no cash value and its premium rises each year, so it guarantees none of the three.","Universal life":"UL's cash value is not guaranteed (interest-sensitive) and premiums are flexible.","Variable life":"VL's cash value varies with the separate-account subaccounts, so it is not guaranteed."},"A single-premium whole life policy is always classified as a:":{"Term policy":"Whole life is permanent with cash value; single-premium funding does not make it term.","Nonqualified annuity":"It is life insurance, not an annuity — different tax and contract rules.","Group policy":"Single-premium whole life is an individual contract, unrelated to group status."},"Under UL Option B (increasing death benefit), the death benefit equals:":{"The cash value only":"The death benefit is NAR plus cash value, not cash value alone.","The net amount at risk only":"Option B adds the cash value on top of the NAR, not NAR by itself.","A level face amount":"That describes Option A (level death benefit); Option B increases as cash value grows."},"Which policy lets the owner direct cash value into subaccounts and requires a prospectus?":{"Whole life":"Whole life has a fixed general-account cash value the owner cannot direct; no prospectus.","Universal life":"UL is interest-sensitive but does not let the owner pick subaccounts and needs no prospectus.","Limited-pay life":"Limited-pay is just whole life paid up early — no separate account, no prospectus."},"Under the incontestability clause, an insurer generally may not contest a policy after:":{"6 months":"Far too short; the standard period is up to 2 years.","1 year in all states":"One year applies in only some states, not all — the maximum standard is 2 years.","5 years":"Exceeds the maximum contestability window; 2 years is the ceiling."},"If a life insurance applicant understated their age, the death benefit will be:":{"Denied entirely":"Age misstatement is not fraud grounds for denial; the benefit is adjusted, not voided.","Paid in full as applied":"Because the premium bought less at the true age, the benefit is reduced accordingly.","Reduced by a 10% penalty":"There is no flat 10% penalty; the benefit is recomputed from what the premium would have purchased."},"Unless the owner elects otherwise, a lapsed whole life policy defaults to which nonforfeiture option?":{"Cash surrender value":"Taking cash surrender must be elected; it is not the automatic default.","Reduced paid-up insurance":"RPU is an available option but not the default; extended term is the default.","Automatic premium loan":"APL is a policy rider that must be selected, not the standard nonforfeiture default."},"Which dividend option generally produces the greatest long-term increase in death benefit and cash value?":{"Cash":"Taking dividends in cash removes them from the policy, adding nothing to death benefit or cash value.","Reduce premium":"Using dividends to lower premiums does not build additional coverage or cash value.","Accumulate at interest":"Accumulating grows cash value modestly but buys no additional insurance, so it lags paid-up additions."},"How are MEC distributions (loans/withdrawals) generally taxed?":{"Tax-free up to basis (FIFO)":"That is the treatment for non-MEC life insurance; MECs are taxed LIFO (gain first).","Long-term capital gains":"MEC gains are ordinary income, not capital gains.","Always fully tax-free":"Only basis is tax-free; MEC gain is taxable, plus a possible pre-59½ penalty."},"Which of the following is a PERMITTED tax-free Section 1035 exchange?":{"Annuity to life insurance":"Not allowed — you cannot exchange an annuity into life insurance under 1035.","Long-term care to life insurance":"Not permitted; LTC can go to LTC or annuity, but not into life insurance.","Annuity to endowment":"Annuity cannot be exchanged into an endowment; the ladder only moves toward annuities/LTC."},"A second-to-die (survivorship) policy is especially useful for:":{"Funding a buy-sell agreement at the first owner's death":"Buy-sell needs cash at the FIRST owner's death; that is first-to-die insurance. Survivorship pays only at the last death.","Covering a home mortgage":"A mortgage needs coverage at the first death; second-to-die pays only when both insureds die.","Short-term income replacement":"Survivorship pays at the last death, useless for immediate income needs — term fits that."},"Which premium method most commonly funds a deferred annuity?":{"Single premium":"Single premium most commonly funds immediate annuities, not deferred ones.","Fixed premium":"Fixed scheduled premiums are not the common deferred-annuity structure; flexibility is the norm.","Modal premium":"Modal refers only to payment frequency, not a funding method for deferred annuities."},"Which annuity payout option provides the maximum guaranteed monthly income for a given sum?":{"Joint and survivor":"J&S covers two lives longer, so it pays the lowest income, not the highest.","Life with 20-year period certain":"Guaranteeing 20 years of payments lowers the monthly amount versus straight life.","Refund annuity":"Guaranteeing a refund of principal reduces the payout below straight life."},"A nonqualified deferred annuity withdrawal is taxed:":{"As return of principal first, then earnings":"That is FIFO; nonqualified annuity withdrawals are LIFO (earnings out first).","As long-term capital gain":"Annuity earnings are ordinary income, never capital gain.","Tax-free in all cases":"Only the return-of-principal portion is tax-free; earnings are taxable."},"The exclusion ratio for an annuitized payment equals:":{"Expected return divided by investment in the contract":"The ratio is inverted — it is investment divided by expected return.","Investment in the contract divided by the annual payment":"That yields a number of years, not the tax-exclusion fraction.","Earnings divided by basis":"Wrong components entirely; the exclusion ratio uses basis over total expected return."},"Compared with an equity-indexed annuity (EIA), a registered index-linked annuity (RILA):":{"Has a higher guaranteed minimum rate":"A RILA has NO guaranteed minimum rate; the EIA is the one with a floor guarantee.","Cannot lose value":"The EIA cannot lose value; a RILA can, via its buffer or floor structure.","Is not regulated by FINRA or the SEC":"RILAs are registered securities subject to SEC/FINRA oversight."},"The most popular annuity living-benefit rider, selected by nearly 70% of buyers, is the:":{"Guaranteed Minimum Income Benefit (GMIB)":"GMIB requires annuitization to access; it is far less popular than the GLWB.","Guaranteed Minimum Accumulation Benefit (GMAB)":"GMAB guarantees principal, not lifetime withdrawals, and is not the top seller.","Guaranteed Minimum Withdrawal Benefit (GMWB)":"GMWB guarantees withdrawals only until principal is recovered, not for life like the GLWB."},"How long must continuation coverage be offered after a divorce makes a spouse lose group health coverage?":{"18 months":"18 months is the COBRA period for employment termination or hour reduction, not divorce.","29 months":"29 months applies to the Social Security disability extension, not divorce.","12 months":"Not a COBRA period; divorce qualifies for 36 months."},"Which event is NOT a COBRA qualifying event?":{"Voluntary termination of employment":"Voluntary termination (not for gross misconduct) is a qualifying event — 18 months.","Reduction to part-time status":"A reduction in hours causing loss of coverage is a qualifying event.","Divorce from the covered employee":"Divorce is a qualifying event giving the ex-spouse 36 months."},"Under the ACA, dependent children may remain on a parent's health plan until age:":{"18":"Too low; the ACA extends coverage well beyond the age of majority.","21":"That is a common trust/support age, not the ACA dependent-coverage age.","30":"Exceeds the ACA limit; coverage ends at 26 regardless of marital or tax status."},"Which managed care plan requires a primary care physician gatekeeper and generally provides no out-of-network coverage except emergencies?":{"PPO":"A PPO needs no gatekeeper and covers out-of-network care at a reduced rate.","Indemnity plan":"Indemnity (fee-for-service) plans have no network restrictions or gatekeeper at all.","POS plan":"A POS uses a gatekeeper but DOES allow out-of-network care (at higher cost), unlike an HMO."},"The Medicaid lookback period for asset transfers is:":{"12 months":"Too short; Medicaid reviews transfers over a 60-month window.","36 months":"36 months was the old rule; the current lookback is 60 months.","No lookback applies":"A lookback definitely applies — five years for uncompensated transfers."},"Inpatient hospital and skilled nursing facility care are covered under which part of Medicare?":{"Part B":"Part B covers physician and outpatient services, not inpatient hospital/SNF stays.","Part C":"Part C (Medicare Advantage) bundles A and B through a private plan; it is not the base inpatient benefit.","Part D":"Part D covers prescription drugs only."},"The Medicare Initial Enrollment Period spans how many months around the 65th birthday?":{"3 months":"Only the months before the birth month; the full IEP is longer.","6 months":"Undercounts; the IEP is 7 months, not 6.","8 months":"8 months is the Special Enrollment Period after losing employer coverage, not the IEP."},"Under 2025 rules, Medicare Part A skilled nursing facility coverage pays in full for the first:":{"100 days":"100 days is the maximum SNF benefit span, but days 21-100 require coinsurance — not paid in full.","60 days":"60 days is the full-coverage period for inpatient hospital, not SNF.","30 days":"Incorrect figure; SNF full coverage is the first 20 days."},"How many standardized Medicare Supplement (Medigap) plans exist?":{"4":"Too few; there are 10 lettered plans (A-N).","6":"Undercounts the standardized plan set of 10.","12":"Overcounts; only 10 standardized Medigap plans exist."},"Starting in 2025, the Inflation Reduction Act caps annual out-of-pocket Medicare Part D drug costs at:":{"$590":"That is roughly the 2025 Part D deductible, not the out-of-pocket cap.","$8,000":"$8,000 was the prior (pre-2025) threshold; the new cap is $2,000.","No cap":"The IRA specifically created a hard $2,000 annual cap."},"Which definition of disability is the broadest and carries the highest premium?":{"Any occupation":"Any-occ is the narrowest, cheapest definition — disabled only if unable to do any job.","Social Security definition":"The SSDI definition is very strict (any substantial gainful activity), the opposite of broad.","Loss of income":"An income-loss (loss-of-earnings) definition pays on income drop but is not the broadest own-occ standard."},"If an employer pays the full premium for a group disability policy, the benefits received are:":{"Tax-free to the employee":"Benefits are tax-free only when the employee paid premiums with after-tax dollars.","Taxed as capital gains":"Disability benefits are ordinary income when taxable, not capital gains.","Tax-free up to 60% of income":"There is no 60%-of-income exclusion; employer-paid benefits are fully taxable."},"A disability policy that is renewable for the full term AND whose premium cannot be changed is:":{"Guaranteed renewable":"Guaranteed renewable locks renewal but the insurer CAN raise premiums for the whole class.","Conditionally renewable":"Renewal depends on stated conditions and premiums can change — weaker than noncancelable.","Optionally renewable":"The insurer chooses whether to renew, so neither renewal nor premium is guaranteed."},"A residual disability benefit pays:":{"50% of the benefit regardless of income loss":"Residual pays proportionally to income lost, not a fixed 50%.","Only a lump sum at recovery":"Residual pays ongoing monthly benefits during partial disability, not a recovery lump sum.","Nothing unless totally disabled":"Residual specifically covers partial disability with actual income loss, not just total disability."},"Under HIPAA, a tax-qualified LTC policy may pay benefits when the insured cannot perform at least how many ADLs?":{"1 of 6":"Too few; the trigger requires an inability to perform at least 2 of the 6 ADLs.","3 of 6":"Overstates the trigger — 2 ADLs, not 3, meets the HIPAA standard.","4 of 6":"Far above the standard; 2 ADLs (or cognitive impairment) is the trigger."},"Which level of care does Medicare NOT cover?":{"Skilled nursing care":"Medicare does cover skilled nursing (post-hospital, limited days).","Intermediate care":"Intermediate skilled care can qualify; custodial care is the excluded type.","Post-hospital home health":"Medicare covers medically necessary post-hospital home health care."},"The single most important LTC insurance rider is generally considered to be:":{"Return of premium":"ROP refunds premiums to a beneficiary but does nothing to protect benefit purchasing power.","Nonforfeiture":"Nonforfeiture preserves some value after lapse but is secondary to inflation protection.","Restoration of benefits":"Restoration restores used benefits after recovery — useful but not the top-ranked rider."},"How are benefits from a tax-qualified LTC policy generally treated for income tax?":{"Fully taxable as ordinary income":"Qualified LTC benefits are generally tax-free, not fully taxable.","Taxed as capital gains":"LTC benefits are not investment gains; qualified benefits are simply excluded.","Taxed only above 7.5% of AGI":"The 7.5% AGI floor applies to the medical-expense deduction, not to LTC benefit taxation."},"Under Section 79, employer-provided group term life is tax-free to the employee up to what amount of coverage?":{"$25,000":"Understates the threshold; the tax-free amount is $50,000 of coverage.","$100,000":"Overstates it — coverage above $50,000 is taxed via Table I.","$2,000":"That is a de minimis figure (e.g., dependent group life), not the $50,000 employee exclusion."},"Employer-paid group health insurance premiums are:":{"Taxable income to the employee and deductible to the employer":"Deductible to the employer is correct, but the premiums are NOT taxable to the employee.","Nondeductible to the employer":"Employer health premiums are an ordinary, deductible business expense.","Taxable to both parties":"Neither party is taxed — deductible to the employer, excluded for the employee."},"A major advantage of a self-funded (ERISA) health plan is:":{"Guaranteed lower claims":"Self-funding does not guarantee lower claims; the employer bears actual claim risk.","No need for stop-loss insurance":"Self-funded plans typically still buy stop-loss to cap catastrophic claims.","State guaranty fund protection":"ERISA plans are exempt from state regulation, so state guaranty funds do not protect them."},"What is the 2025 maximum HSA contribution for family coverage (before catch-up)?":{"$4,300":"That is the 2025 single (self-only) HSA limit, not family.","$6,350":"Not a current HSA figure; the 2025 family limit is $8,550.","$8,300":"That was the 2024 family limit; 2025 rose to $8,550."},"A Section 125 cafeteria plan must offer:":{"Only nontaxable benefits":"A cafeteria plan must include a taxable cash choice, not solely nontaxable benefits.","Long-term care insurance":"LTC insurance is expressly prohibited inside a cafeteria plan.","Educational assistance":"Educational assistance cannot be offered through a Section 125 plan."},"Regarding unused FSA balances, a plan may offer:":{"Both a grace period and a carryover":"A plan may offer one or the other, never both simultaneously.","An unlimited rollover":"Carryover is capped ($660 in 2025), not unlimited.","A cash refund":"Unused FSA funds are forfeited (use-it-or-lose-it), not refunded in cash."},"Workers' compensation insurance is funded by:":{"The employee through payroll deduction":"Employees do not pay for workers' comp; the employer funds it entirely.","Equally split between employer and employee":"It is not shared — the employer bears the full cost.","The state general fund":"Employers (or their insurers/self-funding) pay, not the state general fund."},"With key person life insurance, premiums and the death benefit are treated how?":{"Premiums deductible; death benefit taxable":"Neither part is right — premiums are nondeductible and the benefit is tax-free.","Both deductible and taxable":"Premiums are not deductible, and the death benefit is not taxable.","Premiums deductible; death benefit tax-free":"The death benefit is tax-free, but the premiums are NOT deductible."},"A cross-purchase buy-sell agreement becomes inefficient primarily when:":{"There is only one owner":"A single owner has no co-owner to buy from; cross-purchase presumes multiple owners.","The business is a corporation":"Entity type does not drive cross-purchase inefficiency; the number of owners does.","The owners are related":"Family relationship does not create the policy-count problem; having many owners does."},"Errors and omissions (E&O) insurance is most appropriate for which professionals?":{"Surgeons and dentists":"Physicians and dentists need malpractice (physical-harm) coverage, not E&O.","Manufacturers":"Manufacturers need product-liability coverage, not professional E&O.","Truck drivers":"Truckers need commercial auto/liability coverage, not professional E&O."},"The businessowners policy (BOP) is designed primarily for:":{"Large corporations":"Large firms use the commercial package policy (CPP), not the BOP.","Government entities":"The BOP targets small commercial businesses, not government bodies.","Sole employees only":"The BOP is defined by business size, not headcount of one; it serves small businesses generally."},"A Life Insurance Retirement Plan (LIRP) can be funded with all of the following EXCEPT:":{"Whole life insurance":"Whole life builds cash value and is a valid LIRP funding vehicle.","Universal life insurance":"UL is permanent cash-value coverage, suitable for a LIRP.","A cash value policy":"Any permanent cash-value policy works for a LIRP; that is exactly what is required."},"A client&rsquo;s ability to comprehend and act on financial risk information describes:":{"Risk capacity":"That's the resources/ability to absorb risk (objective), not the ability to understand risk information.","Risk perception":"That's the client's subjective read of how risky something is, not their comprehension of risk data.","Risk tolerance":"That's willingness to accept risk, not the cognitive ability to comprehend and act on risk information."},"The degree to which a client&rsquo;s financial resources can absorb risk is:":{"Risk tolerance":"That's the willingness (subjective) to accept risk, not the objective resource ability to absorb it.","Risk preference":"That's a stated liking for more or less risk, not the resources available to absorb it.","Risk perception":"That's how risky the client feels something is, not their financial ability to absorb risk."},"Which characteristic distinguishes COGNITIVE biases from EMOTIONAL biases?":{"Cognitive biases are permanent":"Backwards — cognitive biases are the correctable ones; emotional biases are the harder, stickier type.","Cognitive biases only affect wealthy clients":"Biases are universal; wealth is irrelevant to whether a bias is cognitive or emotional.","Cognitive biases require medication":"Cognitive biases are corrected with information/coaching, not medical treatment."},"An investor refuses to sell a losing stock because they want to &ldquo;get even&rdquo; first. This is:":{"Overconfidence":"That's overestimating one's own judgment/skill, not the fear-of-loss driving the refusal to sell.","Anchoring":"That's fixating on a reference number (e.g., purchase price) generally, but the driver here is fearing the loss itself.","Recency":"That's overweighting recent events; here the behavior is loss aversion, not reacting to recent news."},"A client keeps a low-yield &ldquo;vacation fund&rdquo; savings account while carrying a 22% credit card balance. This best illustrates:":{"Money illusion":"That's confusing nominal with real (inflation-adjusted) dollars, not siloing money into purpose-based jars.","Herding":"That's following the crowd's actions, not compartmentalizing your own funds by purpose.","Endowment":"That's overvaluing what you own, not treating earmarked savings separately from debt."},"A client only reads news that supports their existing bullish view on a stock. This is:":{"Conservatism":"That's clinging to a prior view despite new info; here the client actively seeks confirming info, which is confirmation bias.","Self-attribution":"That's crediting successes to skill and blaming failures on bad luck, not selectively seeking supportive news.","Framing":"That's deciding differently based on how info is presented, not seeking only confirming information."},"Which is an EMOTIONAL (not cognitive) bias?":{"Anchoring":"Anchoring is a cognitive bias (a reasoning/information error), not an emotional one.","Framing":"Framing is a cognitive bias — an information-processing error, correctable with better presentation.","Conservatism":"Conservatism is a cognitive bias — failing to update on new info, correctable with information."},"A client makes the same choice differently depending only on whether it is described as a &ldquo;90% survival rate&rdquo; vs. &ldquo;10% mortality rate.&rdquo; This is:":{"Recency":"That's overweighting recent events, not choosing differently based on how identical odds are worded.","Herding":"That's following the crowd, not being swayed by the wording/presentation of a choice.","Hindsight":"That's the “I knew it all along” bias about past events, not presentation-driven choice."},"Which counseling approach is described as &ldquo;NOT widely used by planners&rdquo;?":{"Strategic Management":"This approach IS used by planners; the not-widely-used one is Psychoanalytic (Freudian/Gestalt).","Cognitive-Behavioral":"CBT-style approaches ARE commonly used by planners; the outlier is Psychoanalytic.","Economic/Resource":"This is a mainstream planning approach; the not-widely-used one is the Psychoanalytic approach."},"In Social Penetration Theory, which stage involves the FIRST meeting and general conversation?":{"Exploration":"That's a later stage of deeper sharing, not the initial first-meeting stage (Orientation).","Affective Exchange":"That's a deeper stage of significant trust and open disclosure, well past the first meeting.","Stable Exchange":"That's the deepest, established long-term stage, not the initial general-conversation stage."},"A grandmother continually pays the rent of her able-bodied adult grandson, jeopardizing her own retirement. This is best described as:":{"Financial enmeshment":"That's blurring child/parent financial roles (e.g., over-sharing money burdens with kids), not subsidizing a capable adult.","Financial control":"That's using money to dominate or restrict another; here she's supporting, not controlling.","Financial abuse":"That's exploiting someone for financial gain; here the grandmother is the one giving, voluntarily enabling."},"During a market crash a client panics and sells everything, ignoring decades of long-term return history. This reflects:":{"Money illusion":"That's confusing nominal and real dollars, not overreacting to a recent event over long-term data.","Outcome bias":"That's judging a decision only by its result, not overweighting the recent crash.","Status quo":"That's preferring no change/inaction — the opposite of panic-selling everything."},"Which of the following statements is CORRECT regarding financial planning? I. The financial planner is responsible for implementing the client’s Financial Planning recommendation(s) unless specifically excluded from the Scope of Engagement. II. The practitioner must communicate to the client any limitations on the scope of the engagement. III. Recommendations must be written and prepared in a clear, understandable manner. IV. Unrealistic goals must be discussed.":{"I only":"Statements II, III, and IV are also correct duties, so this omits three true items.","I, II, and III":"Leaves out IV — unrealistic goals must be discussed — which is also a correct duty.","II, III, and IV":"Leaves out I — the planner is responsible for implementation unless excluded by the scope of engagement."},"As a member of a team of financial advisors, a financial planner’s responsibilities for a client typically includes which of the following? I. Helping the client identify financial goals II. Preparing tax returns for a small-business owner client III. Analyzing the client’s current financial status IV. Monitoring whether the client is following the plan after implementation":{"I and III":"Omits IV — monitoring the client's progress after implementation is a core planner responsibility.","III and IV":"Omits I — helping the client identify financial goals is a fundamental planner responsibility.","I, II, III, and IV 29":"Includes II — preparing tax returns — which the planner should not do without a PTIN/tax credential."},"In 2014, the average cost of a new home in Kensington Square was $250,000. In 2017, to the disappointment of many prospective homeowners, the average home cost rose to $300,000. Then, in 2019, the average cost of a new home fell to $275,000. The reaction to the decreased cost was positive, though the new average cost was higher than the 2014 average cost of a new home. This behavior is known as":{"money illusion.":"That's confusing nominal vs. real (inflation-adjusted) value, not judging a new price against a prior reference point.","confirmation bias.":"That's seeking info to support an existing belief, not resetting a reference anchor to $300,000.","mental accounting. 49":"That's separating money into purpose-based jars, not evaluating price against a psychological anchor."},"Arnie has accumulated $15,000 in a savings account over the last few years and has earmarked that money to buy a new car. His furnace breaks and requires $9,000 in repairs. Arnie is reluctant to spend the money in his savings account to make the repairs because he wants to use that money for a new car. Instead, he puts the $9,000 repair bill on his credit card at an annual interest rate of 23%. This is an example of which of the following behaviors?":{"Herding":"That's following the actions of a larger group; Arnie is acting alone on his own earmarked funds.","Illusion of control":"That's believing you can control uncontrollable outcomes (e.g., the market), not siloing money by purpose.","Conservatism bias":"That's forming a rational view then failing to update it with new info, not compartmentalizing funds."},"John has a strong feeling about a particular investment’s future performance. He is constantly seeking information to validate his belief that this investment will greatly appreciate. However, he is dismissing any information that is contradictory to his stance. This is an example of which of the following?":{"Framing":"That's choosing differently based on how info is presented, not selectively seeking confirming info.","Recency bias":"That's overweighting recent events, not hunting for information that validates a fixed belief.","Anchoring":"That's fixating on a reference number, not filtering information to support a preexisting view."},"Your client Rosita purchased a stock two years ago at $45 per share, and it is currently trading at $25 per share. Though the prospects for the company do not look good, she does not want to sell the stock until she can at least break even. Rosita is likely exhibiting what emotional bias?":{"Self-control":"That's failing to act in one's long-term interest due to lack of discipline (e.g., under-saving), not refusing to realize a loss.","Status quo":"That's a general preference for inaction; here the specific driver is fear of taking the loss.","Affinity":"That's investing in what reflects one's values/identity, not clinging to a loser to break even."},"Which of the following statements regarding financial conflict is CORRECT?":{"To identify potential financial conflict, financial planners should place more emphasis on what partners discuss than how they discuss it.":"Wrong — planners should give equal weight to what is said AND how it is said.","Nonverbal behaviors, which are important to notice, are not as important as spoken words.":"Wrong — nonverbal cues are important and should not be ranked below spoken words."},"Riva is meeting with Ekram, a financial planner, to review his planning recommendations. During their discussion, Riva explains to Ekram her concern about not having enough money saved for retirement if she follows his recommendations. Meanwhile, Ekram is thinking about a call he received earlier in the day about minor issues his child is having in school. After a minute or so, he realizes he did not hear everything Riva has said to him. He would like to wrap up his meeting with Riva so he can call his son’s teacher. What is the most appropriate action for Ekram?":{"He should try to pay attention and call Riva later for clarification if he missed anything.":"Deferring undermines active listening now; Riva may have said something significant he needs to hear in the moment.","He should ask Riva closed-ended questions to understand her concerns, in an attempt to end the meeting as quickly as possible.":"Rushing with closed-ended questions to end early isn't effective communication and isn't in Riva's best interest.","He should tell Riva that he will adjust his retirement recommendations based on what he’s already heard and end the meeting.":"Acting on partial information he admittedly missed risks bad recommendations and ignores her full concern."},"An investor refuses to sell a losing stock, waiting to \"get even,\" even though better opportunities exist. This best illustrates:":{"Anchoring":"That's fixating on a reference price generally; the core driver here is fearing the loss (loss aversion).","Confirmation bias":"That's seeking info that supports a belief, not the emotional refusal to realize a loss.","Mental accounting":"That's siloing money by purpose, not the aversion to taking a loss on this position."},"How should a planner generally handle a purely EMOTIONAL bias, as compared with a cognitive bias?":{"Correct it with more information and education":"That's how you handle a cognitive bias; emotional biases resist information and usually must be accommodated.","Ignore it":"Biases affecting decisions can't be ignored; emotional ones should be accommodated in the plan.","Report it to a compliance officer":"A behavioral bias isn't a compliance violation; it's addressed through counseling, not reporting."},"A client overloads on employer stock and only reads news supporting that decision. This reflects:":{"Recency bias":"That's overweighting recent events, not selectively seeking info that supports holding employer stock.","Status quo bias":"That's preferring the existing state/inaction, not actively filtering for confirming information.","Endowment bias":"That's overvaluing what you own; here the tell is seeking only supportive news (confirmation bias)."},"A client refuses to use money set aside in a \"vacation fund\" to repair a leaking roof and instead pays penalties to borrow elsewhere. This is:":{"Framing bias":"That's deciding differently based on how a choice is worded, not siloing money into purpose-based jars.","Self-control bias":"That's failing to act in one's long-term interest from lack of discipline, not compartmentalizing funds.","Affinity bias":"That's investing to reflect one's identity/values, not treating earmarked funds as untouchable."},"Automatic enrollment in a retirement plan leverages which behavioral tendency to improve participation?":{"Overconfidence":"That's overrating one's own judgment; defaults work by exploiting inertia, not confidence.","Loss aversion":"That's fearing losses more than valuing gains; auto-enrollment works via inertia/status quo, not loss framing.","Representativeness":"That's judging by stereotypes/resemblance, not the tendency to stick with a default option."},"The degree to which a client's financial resources can absorb and mitigate risk is the client's:":{"Risk tolerance":"That's the willingness (subjective) to accept risk, not the objective resource ability to absorb it.","Risk perception":"That's how risky the client feels something is, not the financial ability to absorb risk.","Risk preference":"That's a stated liking for more or less risk, not the resources available to absorb it."},"Unconscious money beliefs formed in childhood that are resistant to change are called:":{"Mental accounts":"Those are purpose-based mental “jars” for money, not deep-seated childhood money beliefs.","Money illusions":"That's confusing nominal and real dollars, not unconscious childhood-formed money attitudes.","Financial comfort zones":"Not the standard term; the childhood-rooted, resistant beliefs are money scripts."},"A client shows signs of a compulsive-buying disorder affecting their finances. The CFP&reg; professional should:":{"Provide therapy personally":"Planners aren't licensed therapists; providing therapy exceeds their competence and role.","Terminate the relationship immediately":"Abandoning the client isn't appropriate; the proper step is referral to a mental health professional.","Ignore it as outside financial planning":"It materially affects finances and can't be ignored; refer to a financial therapist/mental health professional."},"In Social Penetration Theory, the stage marked by significant trust, open disclosure, and intimacy is:":{"Orientation":"That's the first-meeting, general-conversation stage — well before significant trust develops.","Exploration":"That's a middle stage of increasing but still limited sharing, not deep trust and open disclosure.","Stable Exchange":"That's the deepest, established long-term pattern; the stage first marked by significant trust/open disclosure is Affective Exchange."},"For effective psychological profiling and goal discovery, a planner should primarily use:":{"Closed-ended yes/no questions":"These limit answers to yes/no and yield thin information, poor for rich profiling.","Leading multiple-choice prompts":"Leading prompts steer the client and bias answers, undermining genuine discovery.","Standardized forms only":"Forms alone can't capture the client's own words and nuance needed for profiling."},"A planner's lifelong process of self-evaluating their own beliefs and assumptions about other cultures is:":{"Cultural competency":"That's acquired knowledge about cultures; relied on alone it can breed cultural bias. The ongoing self-evaluation is cultural humility.","Cultural framing":"Not the standard term; the lifelong self-reflection process is cultural humility.","Affinity awareness":"Not the standard term; the ongoing self-examination of one's own cultural assumptions is cultural humility."},"A client demands far more to sell an inherited stock than she would ever pay to buy it. This best illustrates:":{"Anchoring":"That's fixating on a reference number; the gap here comes from overvaluing what she owns (endowment).","Recency bias":"That's overweighting recent events, not valuing an asset more simply because she owns it.","Framing bias":"That's deciding differently based on wording, not the ownership-driven sell-vs-buy price gap."}};
